/* Pharmacology 14–15: Endocrine II; clinical & special pharmacology */
Z("pharm14",0,"ph",["Insulin preparations are classified by onset and duration into rapid-acting (lispro, aspart, glulisine), short-acting (regular), intermediate-acting (NPH) and long-acting (glargine, detemir, degludec); they are used in type 1 diabetes, type 2 diabetes not controlled on oral drugs, DKA, pregnancy and hyperkalaemia.","Basal–bolus insulin mimics the pancreas: long-acting insulin is the steady background 'drip', and rapid-acting insulin is the 'meal-time burst'.",["Rapid-acting analogues (lispro, aspart, glulisine): onset 5–15 min, peak 1 h, duration 3–5 h — just before meals, pumps.","Short-acting (regular/soluble): onset 30 min, peak 2–3 h, duration 6–8 h; only insulin for IV use (DKA, hyperkalaemia, perioperative).","Intermediate (NPH/isophane): onset 2 h, peak 4–10 h, duration 12–18 h; premixed 30/70. Long-acting (glargine, detemir, degludec): peakless basal 20–42 h; ultra-long degludec.","Uses: T1DM (basal–bolus or pump), T2DM (failure of OHAs, HbA1c > 10%, symptomatic, pregnancy, surgery, infection, renal/hepatic failure), GDM, DKA/HHS (IV regular 0.1 U/kg/h), hyperkalaemia (insulin + glucose). ADR: hypoglycaemia (most important), weight gain, lipodystrophy (rotate sites), allergy, insulin resistance, hypokalaemia (IV)."],"Only regular insulin goes IV; glargine is peakless and must not be mixed."],
"Insulin is a polypeptide hormone (51 amino acids: A chain 21, B chain 30, joined by two disulphide bonds) secreted by pancreatic β cells; it acts via the insulin receptor tyrosine kinase to promote glucose uptake (GLUT4) in muscle and fat, glycogen synthesis, lipogenesis and protein synthesis, and to inhibit gluconeogenesis, glycogenolysis, lipolysis and ketogenesis. Sources: formerly bovine and porcine (immunogenic); now human insulin by recombinant DNA technology and insulin analogues with modified amino acid sequences to alter absorption kinetics. Classification by onset and duration: (1) rapid-acting insulin analogues — insulin lispro (Lys-Pro reversal at B28–B29), insulin aspart (B28 Asp), insulin glulisine, faster aspart; do not self-associate into hexamers, so absorbed rapidly from SC tissue: onset 5–15 min, peak 30–90 min, duration 3–5 h; injected immediately before (or just after) meals; used in insulin pumps (CSII); (2) short-acting — regular (soluble, crystalline) human insulin: forms hexamers in SC tissue — onset 30–60 min, peak 2–3 h, duration 6–8 h; injected 30 min before meals; the only insulin suitable for IV infusion (rapid-acting analogues also may be given IV), IM; (3) intermediate-acting — NPH (neutral protamine Hagedorn, isophane) insulin: complex with protamine; cloudy suspension; onset 1–2 h, peak 4–10 h, duration 12–18 h; given twice daily; lente (insulin zinc suspension — obsolete); (4) long-acting basal insulin analogues — insulin glargine (A21 Gly, B30 Arg-Arg; soluble at acidic pH, precipitates at neutral SC pH forming microprecipitates; flat 'peakless' profile; duration ~24 h; once daily; cannot be mixed with other insulins), insulin detemir (fatty acid chain binds albumin; 12–24 h), insulin degludec (multihexamer chains; duration > 42 h; ultra-long; flexible timing; least nocturnal hypoglycaemia), glargine U300; (5) premixed preparations — 30/70 (30% regular + 70% NPH), 50/50, biphasic aspart 30/70, lispro 25/75 — twice daily for convenience in type 2 diabetes; (6) newer — inhaled insulin (Afrezza), once-weekly insulin icodec. Routes: SC (abdomen fastest, arm, thigh, buttock), IV (regular), insulin pens, pumps, inhaled. Factors altering absorption: site, depth, exercise and heat (increase), lipohypertrophy (decrease), dose volume. Regimens: basal–bolus (multiple daily injections — long-acting once daily + rapid-acting before each meal; most physiological; for T1DM and intensive T2DM), split-mixed (premixed or NPH + regular twice daily), basal insulin added to oral agents in T2DM (bedtime NPH or glargine 10 U or 0.1–0.2 U/kg, titrated to fasting glucose), continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion (pump) with continuous glucose monitoring (closed loop). Indications: (1) type 1 diabetes mellitus — mandatory, lifelong; (2) type 2 diabetes — failure of maximal oral therapy, severe hyperglycaemia at diagnosis (HbA1c > 10%, glucose > 300 mg/dL, catabolic symptoms), acute illness, infections, surgery, trauma, MI, stroke, pregnancy (GDM or pre-existing diabetes not controlled by diet), renal or hepatic failure where oral drugs contraindicated, steroid-induced hyperglycaemia; (3) diabetic ketoacidosis and hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state — IV regular insulin infusion 0.1 U/kg/h (after fluids and K⁺ correction), switch to SC when ketoacidosis resolves with overlap; (4) gestational diabetes; (5) perioperative glycaemic control (GIK infusion), critically ill patients (target 140–180 mg/dL); (6) hyperkalaemia — 10 U regular insulin IV with 25–50 g glucose (shifts K⁺ into cells); (7) parenteral nutrition; (8) diagnostic — insulin tolerance test (GH, ACTH reserve). Adverse effects: hypoglycaemia (most common and serious — sweating, tremor, palpitations, hunger, confusion, seizures, coma; masked by β-blockers and in autonomic neuropathy — hypoglycaemia unawareness; treat with oral glucose 15–20 g, or IV 25% dextrose/glucagon 1 mg IM), weight gain, local reactions — lipohypertrophy (repeated injection at the same site; erratic absorption — rotate sites), lipoatrophy (with older impure insulins), local allergy (redness, itching), systemic allergy (rare), insulin antibodies and immunological resistance (> 200 U/day; now rare), insulin oedema (initiation in poorly controlled patients), transient worsening of diabetic retinopathy with rapid control, refraction changes, hypokalaemia (IV insulin, DKA treatment), Somogyi effect (nocturnal hypoglycaemia followed by rebound morning hyperglycaemia — reduce evening NPH) versus dawn phenomenon (early morning hyperglycaemia due to GH/cortisol surge — increase/shift evening insulin). Interactions: β-blockers (mask hypoglycaemia, delay recovery), alcohol (hypoglycaemia), salicylates, ACE inhibitors, MAOIs (potentiate), corticosteroids, thiazides, OCPs, thyroxine, sympathomimetics (antagonise). Storage: unopened vials in refrigerator (2–8 °C, not frozen); in-use vial/pen at room temperature up to 4 weeks; shake NPH/premixed gently (roll); draw regular before NPH when mixing ('clear before cloudy').",
[["Classification of insulin",[["Type","Examples","Onset","Peak","Duration"],["Rapid-acting","Lispro, aspart, glulisine","5–15 min","0.5–1.5 h","3–5 h"],["Short-acting","Regular (soluble)","30–60 min","2–3 h","6–8 h"],["Intermediate","NPH (isophane)","1–2 h","4–10 h","12–18 h"],["Long-acting","Glargine, detemir","1–2 h","Peakless","20–24 h"],["Ultra-long","Degludec, glargine U300","1–2 h","Peakless","> 36–42 h"],["Premixed","30/70, 50/50","30 min","Dual","12–18 h"]]],
["Indications",["Type 1 diabetes","Type 2 diabetes: OHA failure, severe hyperglycaemia, acute illness, surgery","Pregnancy / GDM","DKA, HHS (IV regular)","Renal/hepatic failure","Hyperkalaemia (insulin + glucose)","Parenteral nutrition, ICU"]],
["Adverse effects",[["ADR","Note"],["Hypoglycaemia","Most important; glucose/glucagon"],["Weight gain","Common"],["Lipohypertrophy","Rotate sites"],["Allergy, resistance","Rare with human/analogues"],["Hypokalaemia","IV insulin/DKA"],["Insulin oedema, retinopathy worsening","On rapid control"]]]],
{i:"Modern insulin therapy uses analogues to mimic physiological basal and prandial secretion, with self-monitoring or CGM to balance glycaemic control against hypoglycaemia.",x:[["Somogyi effect vs dawn phenomenon",["Somogyi: nocturnal hypoglycaemia → rebound morning hyperglycaemia → reduce evening dose","Dawn: early morning GH/cortisol surge → hyperglycaemia without nocturnal hypoglycaemia → increase/shift evening dose","Distinguish by 3 am glucose"]]],f:"Basal–bolus regimen:\n Long-acting (glargine/degludec) once daily → steady basal\n + rapid-acting (aspart/lispro) before each meal → prandial peaks\n IV regular insulin → DKA, hyperkalaemia, perioperative",c:"Insulin preparations differ mainly in onset and duration; selecting basal and prandial insulins to mimic physiological secretion, while preventing hypoglycaemia, underpins diabetes management."});

Z("pharm14",1,"ph",["Hypoglycaemia is blood glucose below 70 mg/dL (clinically significant < 54 mg/dL) causing adrenergic and neuroglycopenic symptoms; it is most often due to insulin or sulfonylureas and is treated with fast-acting oral glucose or IV dextrose/glucagon.","The brain runs almost entirely on glucose, so a falling sugar first triggers 'alarm' symptoms (sweating, shaking) and then 'brain fuel failure' (confusion, seizures, coma).",["Causes: drugs (insulin, sulfonylureas — glibenclamide, alcohol, quinine, β-blockers mask), missed meals, exercise, renal/liver failure, insulinoma, adrenal/pituitary insufficiency, sepsis, starvation, post-gastric surgery (dumping), neonatal (IDM).","Symptoms: autonomic — sweating, tremor, palpitations, hunger, anxiety; neuroglycopenic — confusion, behaviour change, slurred speech, diplopia, seizures, coma; Whipple's triad.","Treatment (conscious): 15–20 g fast glucose (glucose tablets, juice, sugar), recheck after 15 min (rule of 15), then complex carbohydrate.","Unconscious: IV 25–50 mL 50% dextrose (children 2–5 mL/kg 10%) then 10% infusion; glucagon 1 mg IM/SC/intranasal (not in alcohol/starvation — no glycogen); sulfonylurea overdose — prolonged glucose infusion + octreotide; thiamine before glucose in alcoholics."],"Hypoglycaemia 'Rule of 15': 15 g glucose, recheck in 15 minutes."],
"Hypoglycaemia is a clinical syndrome with low plasma glucose; in diabetics, level 1 < 70 mg/dL (alert), level 2 < 54 mg/dL (clinically significant), level 3 — severe event requiring assistance; in non-diabetics confirmed by Whipple's triad — symptoms consistent with hypoglycaemia, low plasma glucose measured at the time, and relief of symptoms when glucose is raised. Physiology: as glucose falls, insulin secretion decreases (80 mg/dL), glucagon and adrenaline are released (65–70 mg/dL), then cortisol and GH; symptoms occur at ~55 mg/dL and cognitive dysfunction below 50 mg/dL. Causes: (1) in diabetes — excess insulin or insulin secretagogues (sulfonylureas, especially long-acting glibenclamide, chlorpropamide; meglitinides), missed or delayed meals, unusual exercise, alcohol (inhibits gluconeogenesis), renal failure (reduced insulin and SU clearance), hepatic failure, weight loss, gastroparesis, drug interactions (sulfonamides, fluoroquinolones, fluconazole with sulfonylureas), hypoglycaemia unawareness (recurrent episodes, autonomic neuropathy, β-blockers); (2) in non-diabetics — drugs (insulin factitious, sulfonylureas, alcohol, quinine, pentamidine, salicylates in children, fluoroquinolones, β-blockers, ACE inhibitors), critical illness (sepsis, hepatic, renal, cardiac failure), hormone deficiency (adrenal insufficiency, hypopituitarism, GH deficiency), non-islet cell tumour (IGF-II), endogenous hyperinsulinism — insulinoma, nesidioblastosis, post-bariatric surgery hypoglycaemia, insulin autoimmune syndrome; starvation, malnutrition; inborn errors (glycogen storage disease, galactosaemia, hereditary fructose intolerance); neonatal hypoglycaemia (infants of diabetic mothers, prematurity, IUGR). Clinical features: adrenergic/autonomic — sweating, tremor, palpitations, tachycardia, anxiety, pallor, hunger, paraesthesia; neuroglycopenic — headache, fatigue, difficulty concentrating, confusion, abnormal behaviour (mistaken for drunkenness), slurred speech, blurred/double vision, focal deficits (hemiparesis), seizures, coma, death; nocturnal hypoglycaemia — nightmares, sweating, morning headache. Management: (1) conscious patient able to swallow — 15–20 g of rapid-acting carbohydrate (4 glucose tablets, 150–200 mL fruit juice or regular soft drink, 3–4 teaspoons sugar or honey); recheck blood glucose after 15 minutes; repeat if still < 70 mg/dL (rule of 15); once normal, give a snack with complex carbohydrate and protein to prevent recurrence; (2) unconscious or unable to swallow — IV 50% dextrose 25–50 mL (adults) through a large vein (or 75–100 mL 20%; 10% dextrose 150–200 mL safer for veins), followed by 10% dextrose infusion; in children 2–5 mL/kg of 10% dextrose IV (avoid hypertonic 50%); in alcoholics or malnourished give thiamine 100 mg IV before glucose (prevent Wernicke encephalopathy); (3) glucagon 1 mg IM/SC (0.5 mg in children < 25 kg) or 3 mg intranasal when IV access unavailable (by family members) — mobilises hepatic glycogen; ineffective in starvation, alcohol-induced hypoglycaemia, liver disease, and less effective with sulfonylureas (stimulates insulin); nausea, vomiting; (4) sulfonylurea-induced hypoglycaemia — admit; prolonged observation (24–72 h) with continuous 10% dextrose infusion (recurrence common due to long half-life and active metabolites, especially in elderly and renal impairment); octreotide 50–100 µg SC 6–12-hourly suppresses insulin release (reduces dextrose requirement); diazoxide alternative; (5) treat cause — adjust insulin/SU doses, education, investigate recurrent unexplained hypoglycaemia (72-h fast, insulin, C-peptide, proinsulin, SU screen, β-hydroxybutyrate; imaging for insulinoma); insulinoma — surgery, diazoxide, octreotide; adrenal insufficiency — hydrocortisone. Prevention: patient education (recognise symptoms, carry glucose, wear ID), regular meals, adjust doses for exercise and illness, glucose monitoring (CGM with alarms), choose low-hypoglycaemia drugs (metformin, DPP-4 inhibitors, SGLT2 inhibitors, GLP-1 RAs, analogues like degludec), relaxed targets in elderly, avoid glibenclamide in elderly/CKD, avoid driving when hypoglycaemic.",
[["Causes",[["Category","Examples"],["Drugs","Insulin, sulfonylureas, alcohol, quinine, pentamidine, β-blockers (mask)"],["Diabetes-related","Missed meal, exercise, renal failure, interactions"],["Endocrine","Adrenal insufficiency, hypopituitarism"],["Tumours","Insulinoma, non-islet cell tumour (IGF-II)"],["Others","Sepsis, liver failure, starvation, post-bariatric, inborn errors, neonatal"]]],
["Management",[["Situation","Treatment"],["Conscious","15–20 g fast glucose; recheck 15 min; then snack"],["Unconscious (adult)","50% dextrose 25–50 mL IV → 10% infusion"],["Unconscious (child)","10% dextrose 2–5 mL/kg IV"],["No IV access","Glucagon 1 mg IM/SC or 3 mg intranasal"],["Alcoholic/malnourished","Thiamine before glucose"],["Sulfonylurea overdose","Prolonged dextrose + octreotide; admit 24–72 h"]]]],
{c:"Hypoglycaemia is a medical emergency recognised by autonomic and neuroglycopenic symptoms; prompt glucose, IV dextrose or glucagon reverses it, while prolonged monitoring is needed for sulfonylurea-induced cases and prevention relies on education and drug choice."});

Z("pharm14",2,"ph",["Newer antidiabetic drugs include DPP-4 inhibitors (gliptins), SGLT2 inhibitors (gliflozins) and GLP-1 receptor agonists (glutides); they lower glucose with little hypoglycaemia, and SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 agonists also protect the heart and kidneys and help weight loss.","Incretin drugs boost the gut's own 'meal signal' to release insulin only when glucose is high; SGLT2 inhibitors simply let excess sugar spill out in the urine.",["DPP-4 inhibitors (sitagliptin, vildagliptin, saxagliptin, linagliptin, teneligliptin): prevent breakdown of GLP-1/GIP → glucose-dependent ↑ insulin, ↓ glucagon; oral, weight neutral; ADR — nasopharyngitis, pancreatitis, arthralgia, bullous pemphigoid; linagliptin no renal dose adjustment.","SGLT2 inhibitors (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin, canagliflozin): block glucose reabsorption in PCT → glycosuria (~70 g/day); ↓ HbA1c 0.5–1%, weight, BP; ↓ HF hospitalisation, CKD progression, CV death; ADR — genital mycotic infections, UTI, volume depletion, euglycaemic DKA, Fournier's gangrene, amputation (canagliflozin).","GLP-1 RAs (exenatide, liraglutide, dulaglutide, semaglutide — SC; oral semaglutide; tirzepatide dual GIP/GLP-1): ↑ insulin, ↓ glucagon, slow gastric emptying, satiety → weight loss; ↓ MACE; ADR — nausea, vomiting, pancreatitis, gallstones, medullary thyroid carcinoma (rodents — CI MEN2).","Place: add-on to metformin; choose SGLT2i/GLP-1 RA in ASCVD, HF, CKD, obesity regardless of HbA1c."],"Gliptins = weight neutral; gliflozins = glycosuria + heart/kidney protection; glutides = weight loss."],
"Newer antidiabetic drugs target the incretin system and renal glucose handling. Incretins (GLP-1, glucagon-like peptide-1, and GIP, glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide) are released from intestinal L and K cells after meals, enhancing glucose-dependent insulin secretion, suppressing glucagon, slowing gastric emptying and promoting satiety; they are rapidly degraded (t½ 1–2 min) by dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4); the incretin effect is reduced in type 2 diabetes. (1) DPP-4 inhibitors (gliptins) — sitagliptin, vildagliptin, saxagliptin, linagliptin, alogliptin, teneligliptin, evogliptin (widely used and inexpensive in India); mechanism — competitive inhibition of DPP-4 raising endogenous active GLP-1 and GIP levels 2–3 fold; glucose-dependent insulin release (low hypoglycaemia risk when used alone), reduced glucagon; pharmacokinetics — oral, once daily (vildagliptin twice); renal excretion (dose reduction in CKD) except linagliptin (biliary) and teneligliptin; efficacy — HbA1c reduction 0.5–0.8%; weight neutral; cardiovascular safety neutral (saxagliptin and alogliptin associated with heart failure hospitalisation); uses — type 2 diabetes as monotherapy (metformin intolerance), add-on to metformin, sulfonylurea, insulin; elderly (low hypoglycaemia), CKD (linagliptin); adverse effects — nasopharyngitis, upper respiratory infections, headache, acute pancreatitis (rare), severe arthralgia, bullous pemphigoid, hypersensitivity (angioedema, SJS), hepatotoxicity (vildagliptin — LFT monitoring); hypoglycaemia when combined with SU/insulin. (2) SGLT2 inhibitors (gliflozins) — dapagliflozin, empagliflozin, canagliflozin, ertugliflozin, remogliflozin (India), sotagliflozin (SGLT1/2); mechanism — sodium–glucose cotransporter-2 in the early proximal convoluted tubule reabsorbs ~90% of filtered glucose; inhibition lowers the renal threshold for glucose, causing glycosuria (60–80 g/day, ~240–320 kcal loss) independent of insulin; also natriuresis and osmotic diuresis; restores tubuloglomerular feedback (afferent vasoconstriction — reduced intraglomerular pressure — renoprotection); metabolic shift to ketones; efficacy — HbA1c reduction 0.5–1% (less as eGFR falls), weight loss 2–3 kg, systolic BP reduction 3–5 mmHg, uric acid reduction; outcome benefits (EMPA-REG, CANVAS, DECLARE, DAPA-HF, EMPEROR, DAPA-CKD, CREDENCE) — reduced cardiovascular death and heart failure hospitalisation (also in non-diabetics with HFrEF and HFpEF), slowed CKD progression (also in non-diabetic CKD); uses — type 2 diabetes (add-on to metformin; preferred in heart failure, CKD, ASCVD), heart failure (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin regardless of diabetes), chronic kidney disease (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin); not for type 1 diabetes (DKA risk); adverse effects — genital mycotic infections (vulvovaginal candidiasis, balanitis — most common), urinary tract infections, polyuria, volume depletion, hypotension, acute kidney injury (initial eGFR dip — haemodynamic, reversible), euglycaemic diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA with near-normal glucose — during surgery, fasting, acute illness, insulin reduction; withhold 3 days before surgery and in sick days), Fournier's gangrene (necrotising fasciitis of perineum — rare), lower limb amputation and fractures (canagliflozin), increased LDL, hypoglycaemia with SU/insulin; avoid in eGFR < 20–25 for glycaemic effect (continue for renal/cardiac protection). (3) GLP-1 receptor agonists — short-acting: exenatide (twice daily), lixisenatide; long-acting: liraglutide (daily), dulaglutide (weekly), exenatide ER (weekly), semaglutide (weekly SC; daily oral — first oral GLP-1 RA); dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist: tirzepatide (weekly); mechanism — resist DPP-4 degradation; activate GLP-1 receptors: glucose-dependent insulin secretion, suppression of glucagon, delayed gastric emptying, central appetite suppression (hypothalamus) — weight loss 3–15% (semaglutide/tirzepatide greater); improve β-cell function; efficacy — HbA1c reduction 1–2%; outcome benefits — liraglutide (LEADER), semaglutide (SUSTAIN-6, SELECT — in non-diabetic obese), dulaglutide (REWIND) reduce major adverse cardiovascular events; renal benefit (FLOW); uses — type 2 diabetes (add-on to metformin; preferred with ASCVD, obesity; before insulin or combined with basal insulin), obesity (liraglutide 3 mg, semaglutide 2.4 mg, tirzepatide), NASH, OSA; adverse effects — nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, constipation (dose escalation reduces), early satiety, injection-site reactions, acute pancreatitis, gallbladder disease (cholelithiasis), worsening diabetic retinopathy with rapid control (semaglutide), tachycardia, aspiration risk during anaesthesia (delayed gastric emptying), loss of lean mass; C-cell hyperplasia and medullary thyroid carcinoma in rodents — contraindicated with personal or family history of MTC or MEN2; hypoglycaemia with SU/insulin; antibody formation (exenatide). Other newer agents: amylin analogue (pramlintide), dopamine agonist (bromocriptine QR), bile acid sequestrant (colesevelam), dual PPAR agonist (saroglitazar), glucokinase activator (dorzagliatin), imeglimin. Place in therapy (ADA/EASD 2023/RSSDI): lifestyle + metformin first-line; in patients with established ASCVD, heart failure or CKD, add an SGLT2 inhibitor and/or GLP-1 RA with proven benefit irrespective of HbA1c or metformin use; for weight management, GLP-1 RA or tirzepatide; DPP-4 inhibitors when hypoglycaemia avoidance and cost are priorities (not with GLP-1 RA).",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","DPP-4 inhibitors","SGLT2 inhibitors","GLP-1 RAs"],["Examples","Sitagliptin, vildagliptin, linagliptin, teneligliptin","Dapagliflozin, empagliflozin, canagliflozin","Liraglutide, dulaglutide, semaglutide; tirzepatide"],["Route","Oral","Oral","SC (oral semaglutide)"],["MOA","↑ Endogenous GLP-1/GIP","Glycosuria (PCT)","GLP-1 receptor agonism"],["HbA1c ↓","0.5–0.8%","0.5–1%","1–2%"],["Weight","Neutral","↓ 2–3 kg","↓↓ 3–15%"],["Hypoglycaemia","Low","Low","Low"],["CV outcomes","Neutral","↓ HF, CV death","↓ MACE"],["Renal outcomes","Neutral","↓ CKD progression","Beneficial"],["Key ADR","Pancreatitis, arthralgia, pemphigoid","Genital infections, euglycaemic DKA, volume depletion, Fournier's","Nausea, vomiting, pancreatitis, gallstones; CI MEN2/MTC"]]],
["Preferred choice",[["Comorbidity","Preferred add-on"],["Heart failure","SGLT2 inhibitor"],["CKD","SGLT2 inhibitor (± GLP-1 RA)"],["ASCVD","GLP-1 RA or SGLT2 inhibitor"],["Obesity","GLP-1 RA / tirzepatide"],["Elderly, hypoglycaemia risk, cost","DPP-4 inhibitor"]]]],
{i:"The newer agents shifted diabetes care from glucose-centric to organ-protective therapy.",f:"Meal → gut L cells → GLP-1 → (DPP-4 degrades ← gliptins block) → β-cell insulin ↑, glucagon ↓, gastric emptying ↓, satiety ↑\n GLP-1 RAs: resistant to DPP-4 → stronger action\n Kidney PCT: SGLT2 reabsorbs glucose ← gliflozins block → glycosuria, natriuresis",c:"DPP-4 inhibitors offer weight-neutral, low-hypoglycaemia glucose control, while SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 receptor agonists add weight loss and proven cardiorenal protection, shaping modern type 2 diabetes management."});

Z("pharm14",3,"ph",["Pituitary and hypothalamic drugs mimic or block pituitary hormones: growth hormone (somatropin) treats GH deficiency; octreotide (somatostatin analogue) treats acromegaly, carcinoid and variceal bleeding; desmopressin (ADH V2 analogue) treats diabetes insipidus, nocturnal enuresis and bleeding disorders.","These drugs are hormone 'stand-ins': somatropin replaces GH, octreotide copies the body's 'stop' hormone somatostatin, and desmopressin copies ADH's water-saving action without raising BP.",["GH (somatropin, recombinant): uses — GH deficiency in children (short stature), Turner, Prader–Willi, chronic renal failure, SGA, adult GH deficiency, HIV wasting; ADR — oedema, arthralgia, carpal tunnel, hyperglycaemia, intracranial hypertension, SCFE; GH antagonist pegvisomant (acromegaly); mecasermin (IGF-1).","Octreotide/lanreotide: SST2/5 agonists, ↓ GH, insulin, glucagon, gastrin, VIP, serotonin, splanchnic blood flow; uses — acromegaly, carcinoid syndrome, VIPoma, gastrinoma, variceal bleeding, secretory diarrhoea, sulfonylurea hypoglycaemia, TSHoma; ADR — GI upset, gallstones, hyper/hypoglycaemia, bradycardia.","Desmopressin (dDAVP): selective V2 agonist, long-acting, no vasopressor effect; uses — central DI (intranasal/oral), nocturnal enuresis, haemophilia A (mild) and von Willebrand type 1 (↑ factor VIII, vWF), uraemic bleeding; ADR — hyponatraemia, water intoxication. Vasopressin/terlipressin (V1) — variceal bleeding, hepatorenal syndrome, shock.","Others: GnRH analogues (leuprolide — continuous → suppression: prostate cancer, endometriosis, precocious puberty; pulsatile → infertility), bromocriptine/cabergoline (prolactinoma, acromegaly), ACTH/tetracosactide (diagnostic), oxytocin."],"Desmopressin = V2 (water) only; terlipressin = V1 (vessels)."],
"Hypothalamic and anterior/posterior pituitary hormones and their analogues are used for replacement, suppression and diagnosis. Growth hormone (somatotropin): 191-amino acid polypeptide secreted by somatotrophs under GHRH (stimulatory) and somatostatin (inhibitory) control; acts on GH receptors (JAK2–STAT) directly (lipolysis, anti-insulin, Na⁺ retention) and via hepatic IGF-1 (growth of bone, cartilage, muscle, organs); recombinant human GH (somatropin) given SC daily (long-acting weekly — somapacitan, lonapegsomatropin); uses — GH deficiency in children (short stature with proven deficiency; most effective before epiphyseal closure), Turner syndrome, Prader–Willi syndrome, Noonan syndrome, chronic kidney disease, children born small for gestational age without catch-up growth, idiopathic short stature (some countries), SHOX deficiency; adult GH deficiency (improves body composition, bone density, quality of life); HIV-associated wasting, short bowel syndrome; misuse in sports/anti-ageing (unethical); adverse effects — peripheral oedema, arthralgia, myalgia, carpal tunnel syndrome, insulin resistance and hyperglycaemia, benign intracranial hypertension, slipped capital femoral epiphysis, scoliosis progression, gynaecomastia, hypothyroidism unmasking, possible tumour growth concern; contraindicated in active malignancy, closed epiphyses (for growth), critical illness, proliferative retinopathy. GH antagonists and related: pegvisomant (GH receptor antagonist — acromegaly resistant to somatostatin analogues), mecasermin (recombinant IGF-1 — primary IGF-1 deficiency/Laron syndrome), GHRH analogue tesamorelin (HIV lipodystrophy). Somatostatin and analogues: somatostatin (14-amino acid peptide from hypothalamus, pancreatic δ cells and gut) inhibits release of GH, TSH, insulin, glucagon, gastrin, secretin, CCK, VIP, serotonin, gastric acid and pancreatic enzymes, reduces splanchnic and portal blood flow and GI motility; very short t½ (2–3 min) — limited use (IV infusion in variceal bleeding); octreotide — synthetic octapeptide analogue, 40 times more potent on GH, t½ 90 min, given SC 50–200 µg 8-hourly or IV infusion; long-acting release (LAR) IM monthly; lanreotide (deep SC monthly), pasireotide (broader receptor profile — Cushing disease); mechanism — agonists at somatostatin receptors (mainly SSTR2 and SSTR5); uses — acromegaly (reduce GH and IGF-1, tumour shrinkage; before/after surgery or when surgery fails), TSH-secreting adenoma, neuroendocrine tumours — carcinoid syndrome (flushing, diarrhoea; prevention of carcinoid crisis during surgery), VIPoma (watery diarrhoea), glucagonoma, gastrinoma, insulinoma (variable); acute variceal bleeding (reduces portal pressure; with endoscopic therapy; 50 µg bolus then 50 µg/h for 2–5 days), secretory diarrhoea (AIDS-related, chemotherapy-induced, dumping syndrome, short bowel, ileostomy output), sulfonylurea-induced hypoglycaemia, enterocutaneous and pancreatic fistulae, post-pancreatic surgery complications, chylothorax, hepatorenal syndrome (with midodrine); radiolabelled analogues for imaging (68Ga-DOTATATE PET) and therapy (177Lu-DOTATATE — PRRT); adverse effects — abdominal cramps, nausea, flatulence, diarrhoea, steatorrhoea, gallstones and sludge (inhibits gallbladder contraction — 20–30% with long-term), hyperglycaemia or hypoglycaemia, bradycardia, conduction abnormalities, hypothyroidism, injection-site pain, vitamin B12 deficiency. Desmopressin (1-deamino-8-D-arginine vasopressin, dDAVP): synthetic analogue of ADH (vasopressin) — deamination increases duration (t½ 1.5–3 h; action 8–20 h) and D-arginine substitution removes V1 (vasopressor) activity, making it a selective V2 receptor agonist (antidiuretic/vasopressor ratio 3000:1 vs 1:1 for vasopressin); V2 activation in collecting duct principal cells increases cAMP and insertion of aquaporin-2 water channels — antidiuresis; V2 on endothelium releases von Willebrand factor and factor VIII; routes — intranasal spray (10–40 µg/day), oral tablets (0.1–0.4 mg; low bioavailability), sublingual melt, SC/IV (1–4 µg); uses — (1) central (neurogenic, pituitary) diabetes insipidus — drug of choice for long-term therapy; test to distinguish central from nephrogenic DI (ineffective in nephrogenic); (2) primary nocturnal enuresis in children (> 5–6 years) — at bedtime with fluid restriction; nocturia in adults (nocturnal polyuria); (3) haemostatic — mild haemophilia A and type 1 von Willebrand disease (0.3 µg/kg IV/SC or intranasal 300 µg before minor surgery, dental extraction; increases factor VIII and vWF 2–5 fold; tachyphylaxis with repeated doses), uraemic bleeding, platelet dysfunction (antiplatelet drugs, cirrhosis), haemorrhage during cardiac surgery; (4) diagnostic — renal concentrating capacity test; adverse effects — hyponatraemia and water intoxication (headache, nausea, confusion, seizures — especially children, elderly, polydipsia; restrict fluids, monitor Na⁺), facial flushing, headache, nasal irritation, abdominal cramps, rarely thrombosis (vWF release); contraindicated in type 2B von Willebrand disease (thrombocytopenia), habitual polydipsia, heart failure. Vasopressin (arginine vasopressin) and terlipressin (prodrug, V1 predominant, longer-acting): V1 receptor — splanchnic vasoconstriction, used in oesophageal variceal bleeding (terlipressin 2 mg IV 4-hourly), hepatorenal syndrome (terlipressin + albumin), vasodilatory/septic shock (vasopressin 0.03 U/min with noradrenaline), cardiac arrest (historical), central DI (vasopressin short-acting); adverse effects — ischaemia (coronary, mesenteric, peripheral), hypertension, bradycardia, hyponatraemia. Vaptans (V2 antagonists — tolvaptan, conivaptan) for SIADH/euvolaemic hyponatraemia and ADPKD. Other hypothalamic–pituitary drugs: GnRH agonists (leuprolide, goserelin, triptorelin — pulsatile for infertility/delayed puberty; continuous for desensitisation in prostate and breast cancer, endometriosis, uterine fibroids, precocious puberty, IVF protocols), GnRH antagonists (degarelix, cetrorelix, ganirelix, elagolix, relugolix), dopamine agonists (bromocriptine, cabergoline — hyperprolactinaemia/prolactinoma, acromegaly, suppression of lactation), gonadotropins (FSH, hCG — ovulation induction, cryptorchidism), TRH and CRH (diagnostic), corticotropin/tetracosactide (cosyntropin — Synacthen test for adrenal insufficiency; infantile spasms), oxytocin (labour).",
[["Key drugs",[["Drug","Mechanism","Uses","Key ADR"],["Somatropin (GH)","GH receptor → IGF-1","GH deficiency, Turner, PWS, CKD, SGA, adult GHD","Oedema, arthralgia, hyperglycaemia, IIH, SCFE"],["Octreotide/lanreotide","SSTR2/5 agonist","Acromegaly, carcinoid, VIPoma, variceal bleed, secretory diarrhoea, SU hypoglycaemia","GI upset, gallstones, dysglycaemia, bradycardia"],["Pegvisomant","GH receptor antagonist","Acromegaly","Raised LFTs"],["Desmopressin","Selective V2 agonist","Central DI, enuresis, haemophilia A, vWD type 1, uraemic bleeding","Hyponatraemia, water intoxication"],["Terlipressin/vasopressin","V1 agonist","Variceal bleed, HRS, septic shock","Ischaemia, hypertension"],["GnRH agonists","Continuous → desensitisation","Prostate cancer, endometriosis, precocious puberty","Flare, hot flushes, bone loss"],["Cabergoline/bromocriptine","D2 agonist","Prolactinoma, acromegaly, lactation suppression","Nausea, hypotension, valvulopathy (high dose)"]]],
["Desmopressin vs vasopressin",[["Feature","Desmopressin","Vasopressin"],["Receptor","V2 selective","V1 + V2"],["Duration","8–20 h","2–8 h"],["Vasopressor effect","Negligible","Marked"],["Main uses","DI, enuresis, bleeding disorders","Variceal bleed, septic shock"]]]],
{c:"Pituitary-related drugs replace deficient hormones (GH, desmopressin) or suppress excess (octreotide, pegvisomant, dopamine agonists); their receptor selectivity determines uses — desmopressin's V2 selectivity suits diabetes insipidus and bleeding disorders, while somatostatin analogues treat acromegaly, neuroendocrine tumours and variceal bleeding."});

Z("pharm14",4,"ph",["Uterine stimulants (oxytocics) increase uterine contractions: oxytocin is used to induce/augment labour and prevent postpartum haemorrhage (PPH); ergometrine, misoprostol and carboprost are used mainly to control PPH, and prostaglandins also ripen the cervix and induce abortion.","Oxytocin produces rhythmic, physiological contractions (like natural labour), whereas ergometrine causes sustained contraction — perfect to clamp bleeding vessels after delivery but dangerous before the baby is out.",["Oxytocin: OT receptors (Gq → ↑ Ca²⁺); IV infusion (2–5 mU/min titrated) for induction/augmentation; 10 IU IM in third stage (AMTSL — drug of choice for PPH prevention); milk ejection; ADR — uterine hyperstimulation, fetal distress, rupture, water intoxication (ADH-like), hypotension (bolus).","Ergometrine (methylergometrine): α-adrenergic/5-HT agonist → sustained tetanic contraction; 0.2 mg IM/IV — PPH treatment/prevention after delivery; CI — hypertension, pre-eclampsia, heart disease, before delivery of baby (and twins); ADR — hypertension, vomiting, vasospasm, gangrene.","Misoprostol (PGE1): oral/sublingual/rectal/vaginal; PPH (600 µg oral prevention; 800 µg SL treatment) where oxytocin unavailable; cervical ripening/induction (25 µg vaginal), medical abortion (with mifepristone), missed abortion; ADR — shivering, fever, diarrhoea; NSAID ulcer prevention.","Carboprost (15-methyl PGF2α): 250 µg IM every 15 min (max 2 mg) — refractory PPH due to atony; CI asthma (bronchospasm); dinoprostone (PGE2) gel — cervical ripening; tocolytics oppose (nifedipine, atosiban)."],"PPH drugs: Oxytocin → Ergometrine → Carboprost → Misoprostol (+ tranexamic acid)."],
"Uterine stimulants (oxytocics, ecbolics) are drugs that increase the force and frequency of uterine contractions. Classification: (1) posterior pituitary hormone — oxytocin; carbetocin (heat-stable long-acting analogue); (2) ergot alkaloids — ergometrine (ergonovine), methylergometrine; (3) prostaglandins — PGE2: dinoprostone; PGF2α: dinoprost, carboprost (15-methyl PGF2α); PGE1: misoprostol, gemeprost; (4) miscellaneous — antiprogestin mifepristone (sensitises uterus to PGs), ethacridine lactate (extra-amniotic). Oxytocin: nonapeptide synthesised in the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei, stored in posterior pituitary; released by cervical/vaginal stretch (Ferguson reflex) and suckling; mechanism — binds Gq-coupled oxytocin receptors on myometrium (receptor density increases markedly near term due to oestrogen), activating phospholipase C → IP3 → Ca²⁺ release and influx → contraction; also stimulates PG release from decidua; actions — at low doses increases frequency and force of rhythmic contractions with complete relaxation in between (physiological, fundal dominance, cervical dilatation); high doses cause sustained contraction (tetany); contracts myoepithelial cells of breast (milk ejection — 'let-down'); weak ADH-like action at high doses (water retention); transient vasodilatation and hypotension with IV bolus; pharmacokinetics — inactivated orally; given IV (infusion), IM; t½ 3–5 min; uses — (a) induction of labour (post-term, PROM, pre-eclampsia, IUGR, diabetes, IUFD) and augmentation of slow/prolonged labour — dilute IV infusion starting 1–2 mU/min, increasing every 30 min until 3–4 contractions per 10 minutes (max 20–32 mU/min) with continuous fetal and uterine monitoring; (b) active management of third stage of labour (AMTSL) and prevention of PPH — 10 IU IM (or slow IV) immediately after delivery of the baby (WHO drug of choice; carbetocin heat-stable alternative); (c) treatment of PPH due to uterine atony — 20–40 IU in 1 L saline infusion; (d) after caesarean section, incomplete/missed abortion, molar evacuation; (e) oxytocin challenge test (fetal reserve); (f) breast engorgement (nasal spray — historical); adverse effects — uterine hyperstimulation (tachysystole), fetal hypoxia and distress, uterine rupture (especially in grand multipara, previous caesarean scar, obstructed labour, malpresentation), placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism, water intoxication and hyponatraemia (prolonged high-dose infusion in hypotonic fluids — convulsions, coma), hypotension and tachycardia (rapid IV bolus), neonatal jaundice; contraindications — cephalopelvic disproportion, malpresentation, placenta praevia, fetal distress, previous classical caesarean, obstructed labour, hypertonic uterus. Ergometrine and methylergometrine: amine ergot alkaloids; mechanism — partial agonists at α-adrenergic, 5-HT2 and dopamine receptors in uterine and vascular smooth muscle; produce powerful sustained (tonic) uterine contractions with increased basal tone (rather than rhythmic), especially in the puerperal uterus — compress blood vessels traversing myometrium (living ligatures); vasoconstriction; rapid onset (IV 1 min, IM 2–5 min, oral 10 min), duration 3–6 h; dose 0.2 mg IM/IV (repeat every 2–4 h, max 1 mg); uses — prevention and treatment of postpartum haemorrhage (after delivery of the baby and placenta; often as syntometrine — oxytocin 5 IU + ergometrine 0.5 mg IM), subinvolution of uterus, after evacuation of products of conception; migraine diagnosis (historical); adverse effects — nausea, vomiting, hypertension (vasoconstriction — dangerous in pre-eclampsia), headache, coronary vasospasm, chest pain, peripheral vasospasm and gangrene (with prolonged use, CYP3A4 inhibitors — macrolides, HIV protease inhibitors), retained placenta (if given before third stage — cervical spasm); contraindications — use before delivery of the baby (sustained contraction causes fetal asphyxia), undiagnosed twin pregnancy, induction/augmentation of labour, hypertension, pre-eclampsia/eclampsia, heart disease, peripheral vascular disease, severe hepatic/renal disease, sepsis, porphyria. Prostaglandins: PGE2 and PGF2α contract the pregnant uterus at all stages (unlike oxytocin, effective in early pregnancy) and soften/ripen the cervix (collagen breakdown); misoprostol (synthetic PGE1 analogue; stable at room temperature, cheap, oral/sublingual/buccal/vaginal/rectal) — uses: prevention of PPH (600 µg oral) and treatment (800 µg sublingual) when oxytocin is unavailable (community, home births), cervical ripening and induction of labour at term (25 µg vaginal every 4–6 h or 25 µg oral every 2 h), medical termination of pregnancy (with mifepristone 200 mg followed 24–48 h later by misoprostol 800 µg vaginal/buccal — up to 9 weeks and beyond as per MTP Act), missed and incomplete abortion, cervical priming before surgical abortion/hysteroscopy, NSAID-induced ulcer prophylaxis; adverse effects — shivering, pyrexia (dose-related), nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, abdominal cramps, uterine hyperstimulation and rupture (especially previous caesarean scar), teratogenicity (Möbius syndrome, limb defects if pregnancy continues); carboprost (15-methyl PGF2α; 250 µg deep IM, repeat every 15–90 min to max 2 mg (8 doses), also intramyometrial) — second-line for PPH due to uterine atony refractory to oxytocin and ergometrine, second-trimester abortion; adverse effects — vomiting, diarrhoea, fever, flushing, bronchospasm (contraindicated in asthma), hypertension, pulmonary oedema; dinoprostone (PGE2) — intracervical gel 0.5 mg or vaginal gel/pessary (sustained-release insert 10 mg) for cervical ripening and induction of labour; also extra-amniotic for second trimester abortion; vomiting, diarrhoea, hyperstimulation (insert removable). Management of PPH (stepwise): call for help, uterine massage, IV access, fluids, blood; oxytocin infusion → ergometrine (if no hypertension) → carboprost (if no asthma) → misoprostol (rectal/sublingual); tranexamic acid 1 g IV within 3 hours of birth (WOMAN trial — reduces death from bleeding); balloon tamponade, uterine compression sutures (B-Lynch), artery ligation, embolisation, hysterectomy. Tocolytics (uterine relaxants) for preterm labour — nifedipine, atosiban (oxytocin antagonist), indomethacin, β2 agonists (ritodrine, terbutaline), magnesium sulphate (neuroprotection).",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs"],["Oxytocin and analogue","Oxytocin, carbetocin"],["Ergot alkaloids","Ergometrine, methylergometrine"],["Prostaglandins","Misoprostol (PGE1), dinoprostone (PGE2), carboprost (15-methyl PGF2α)"],["Others","Mifepristone (sensitiser), ethacridine"]]],
["Oxytocin vs ergometrine",[["Feature","Oxytocin","Ergometrine"],["Contraction type","Rhythmic, physiological","Sustained, tonic"],["Use in labour","Induction, augmentation","Never before delivery"],["PPH","Prevention (drug of choice) and treatment","Prevention and treatment"],["Onset (IM)","2–3 min","2–5 min"],["CVS","Hypotension (bolus)","Hypertension"],["Water intoxication","Yes (high dose)","No"],["CI","CPD, malpresentation, previous classical CS","Hypertension, pre-eclampsia, heart disease, before baby delivered"]]],
["PPH drug sequence",[["Step","Drug and dose"],["1","Oxytocin 10 IU IM / 20–40 IU infusion"],["2","Ergometrine/methylergometrine 0.2 mg IM/IV (no hypertension)"],["3","Carboprost 250 µg IM every 15 min, max 2 mg (no asthma)"],["4","Misoprostol 800 µg SL/rectal"],["Adjunct","Tranexamic acid 1 g IV within 3 h"]]]],
{i:"PPH is a leading cause of maternal death in India; routine oxytocin in the third stage and a stepwise uterotonic protocol save lives.",f:"Labour: oxytocin infusion → rhythmic contractions\n Cervical ripening: dinoprostone / misoprostol\n Third stage: oxytocin 10 IU IM (AMTSL)\n PPH (atony): oxytocin → ergometrine → carboprost → misoprostol + tranexamic acid → surgery",c:"Oxytocin is the uterotonic of choice for labour induction and PPH prevention, ergometrine gives sustained contraction after delivery but is contraindicated in hypertension, and prostaglandins (misoprostol, carboprost, dinoprostone) serve cervical ripening, abortion and refractory PPH."});

Z("pharm14",5,"ph",["Oestrogens, progestins and androgens are sex steroids used for contraception, hormone replacement, gynaecological disorders and hypogonadism; their antagonists and modulators (SERMs, antiprogestins, antiandrogens) treat cancers and other conditions.","Oestrogen builds (endometrium, bone, female features), progesterone prepares and stabilises (secretory endometrium, pregnancy maintenance), androgen masculinises and builds muscle.",["Oestrogens: estradiol, conjugated oestrogens, ethinylestradiol (OCPs), estriol; uses — contraception, menopausal HRT (hot flushes, atrophic vaginitis, osteoporosis prevention), hypogonadism (Turner), prostate cancer (historical); ADR — nausea, breast tenderness, VTE, endometrial cancer (unopposed), breast cancer (combined HRT), gallstones, hypertension, migraine.","Progestins: progesterone, medroxyprogesterone, norethisterone, levonorgestrel, desogestrel, dienogest, drospirenone; uses — contraception (POP, injectable DMPA, implant, LNG-IUS, emergency 1.5 mg LNG), HRT (endometrial protection), dysfunctional uterine bleeding, endometriosis, threatened/recurrent abortion, preterm birth prevention, endometrial cancer; ADR — irregular bleeding, weight gain, mood change, acne, lipid change.","Androgens: testosterone (gel, injection — enanthate, undecanoate), mesterolone, anabolic steroids (nandrolone, oxandrolone); uses — male hypogonadism, delayed puberty, osteoporosis in men, anaemia (CKD, aplastic), cachexia; ADR — acne, polycythaemia, prostate enlargement, ↓ spermatogenesis, virilisation in women, hepatotoxicity/peliosis (17α-alkyl), dyslipidaemia; abuse.","Modulators: SERMs (tamoxifen, raloxifene, clomiphene), aromatase inhibitors (letrozole), antiprogestin (mifepristone, ulipristal), antiandrogens (flutamide, bicalutamide, cyproterone, spironolactone), 5α-reductase inhibitors (finasteride)."],"OCP serious risk: VTE (↑ with smoking > 35 years)."],
"Sex steroids are synthesised from cholesterol in gonads, adrenals and placenta; they act through intracellular nuclear receptors (ERα/β, PR-A/B, AR) that regulate gene transcription, and also rapid non-genomic effects. Oestrogens: natural — estradiol (most potent), estrone, estriol (pregnancy); synthetic steroidal — ethinylestradiol (orally active, used in OCPs), mestranol, conjugated equine oestrogens, estradiol valerate; non-steroidal — diethylstilbestrol (DES — vaginal adenocarcinoma in daughters; obsolete), hexestrol; physiological actions — development of female secondary sex characteristics, proliferation of endometrium (proliferative phase), cervical mucus thin and watery, vaginal epithelial cornification, breast duct growth, maintenance of bone mass (inhibit osteoclast resorption — closure of epiphyses), favourable lipid profile (↑ HDL, ↓ LDL), increased hepatic synthesis of binding globulins (SHBG, TBG, CBG), clotting factors (II, VII, IX, X) and renin substrate (hypertension), salt and water retention, cholesterol saturation of bile (gallstones), feedback inhibition of FSH (and LH); routes — oral, transdermal patch/gel (bypass first pass — less VTE and hepatic effects), vaginal cream/ring/tablet, implants, injection; uses — (1) hormonal contraception (with progestins), (2) menopausal hormone therapy — vasomotor symptoms (hot flushes, night sweats), genitourinary syndrome of menopause (vaginal oestrogen), prevention of osteoporosis in women at high risk who cannot take alternatives, premature ovarian insufficiency (until average age of menopause); in women with uterus always combined with a progestin (to prevent endometrial hyperplasia/carcinoma); lowest effective dose for shortest time; (3) hypogonadism — Turner syndrome, primary ovarian failure (induction of puberty), (4) dysfunctional uterine bleeding (high-dose IV conjugated oestrogen for acute bleeding), (5) atrophic vaginitis, senile vaginitis, (6) prostate cancer (historical), (7) acne and hirsutism (in OCPs with antiandrogenic progestin), (8) gender-affirming hormone therapy; adverse effects — nausea, breast tenderness and enlargement, headache, migraine, fluid retention, oedema, weight gain, hypertension, venous thromboembolism (DVT, PE — increased with smoking, obesity, immobilisation, thrombophilia — factor V Leiden), stroke, cholestatic jaundice, gallstones, hepatic adenoma, endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma (unopposed oestrogen), breast cancer (combined HRT, WHI study), melasma, hypertriglyceridaemia (oral), worsening of SLE, fibroids; in males — gynaecomastia, impotence, feminisation; contraindications — oestrogen-dependent cancers (breast, endometrium), undiagnosed vaginal bleeding, active/past VTE, thrombophilia, active liver disease, stroke/MI, pregnancy, porphyria, uncontrolled hypertension, smokers > 35 years (OCP). Progestins: natural progesterone (micronised oral, vaginal gel/pessaries, IM in oil), 17α-hydroxyprogesterone caproate; synthetic — pregnane derivatives (medroxyprogesterone acetate, megestrol, dydrogesterone, cyproterone acetate — antiandrogenic, chlormadinone), estrane/19-nortestosterone derivatives (norethisterone, lynestrenol), gonane derivatives (levonorgestrel, norgestrel, desogestrel, gestodene, norgestimate, dienogest), spironolactone derivative (drospirenone — antimineralocorticoid, antiandrogenic); physiological actions — secretory transformation of oestrogen-primed endometrium, maintenance of pregnancy (quiescence of myometrium), thick viscid cervical mucus (impenetrable to sperm), alveolar breast development, raises basal body temperature, feedback inhibition of LH (ovulation suppression), respiratory stimulation, some androgenic (19-nortestosterone derivatives), anti-oestrogenic and glucocorticoid effects (MPA); uses — (1) contraception — combined OCPs, progestin-only pills (norethisterone 0.35 mg, desogestrel 75 µg), injectable DMPA 150 mg IM every 3 months (Antara programme), implants (etonogestrel), levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system (also for menorrhagia), emergency contraception (levonorgestrel 1.5 mg within 72 h; ulipristal within 120 h); (2) HRT in women with intact uterus (endometrial protection); (3) dysfunctional uterine bleeding/heavy menstrual bleeding (cyclic norethisterone, LNG-IUS); (4) endometriosis (dienogest, norethisterone, DMPA); (5) secondary amenorrhoea (progesterone challenge test), premenstrual syndrome (drospirenone); (6) threatened and recurrent abortion (luteal phase defect — micronised progesterone, dydrogesterone; evidence moderate), luteal support in IVF, prevention of preterm birth in women with short cervix (vaginal progesterone) or previous preterm birth (17-OHPC); (7) endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma, breast cancer (megestrol), renal cell carcinoma (palliative); (8) appetite stimulation in cachexia (megestrol); (9) delaying menstruation (norethisterone); adverse effects — irregular breakthrough bleeding/spotting, amenorrhoea (DMPA), weight gain, bloating, breast tenderness, mood changes, depression, headache, acne, hirsutism and androgenic effects (older 19-nortestosterone progestins), reduced HDL, reduced bone mineral density (long-term DMPA — reversible), delayed return of fertility (DMPA up to 10 months), increased VTE (drospirenone, desogestrel in combined pills), masculinisation of female fetus (androgenic progestins in early pregnancy — avoided). Androgens: natural — testosterone (from Leydig cells; converted to more potent dihydrotestosterone by 5α-reductase and to estradiol by aromatase); preparations — testosterone esters for IM injection (enanthate, cypionate every 2–4 weeks; undecanoate every 10–12 weeks), transdermal gels and patches, buccal, oral testosterone undecanoate (lymphatic absorption), 17α-alkylated oral androgens (methyltestosterone, fluoxymesterone, danazol — hepatotoxic), mesterolone; anabolic steroids (higher anabolic:androgenic ratio) — nandrolone decanoate, oxandrolone, stanozolol, oxymetholone; physiological actions — male sexual differentiation (DHT — external genitalia, prostate), puberty (genital growth, deep voice, facial/body hair, libido), spermatogenesis (high intratesticular testosterone with FSH), anabolic — increased muscle mass and strength, nitrogen retention, bone growth and epiphyseal closure, erythropoiesis (↑ erythropoietin), sebaceous gland activity (acne), male pattern baldness, feedback inhibition of LH/FSH; uses — (1) male hypogonadism — primary (Klinefelter, orchitis, anorchia) and secondary (hypopituitarism) — replacement therapy; (2) delayed puberty in boys (short course), micropenis; (3) osteoporosis in hypogonadal men; (4) anaemias — aplastic anaemia, myelofibrosis, CKD (largely replaced by erythropoietin), (5) catabolic states, cachexia (cancer, HIV, burns), muscle wasting (oxandrolone), (6) hereditary angioedema (danazol, stanozolol — increase C1 esterase inhibitor), (7) endometriosis and fibrocystic breast disease (danazol), (8) breast cancer (historical), (9) gender-affirming therapy (transgender men), (10) hypoactive sexual desire in postmenopausal women (selected); adverse effects — virilisation in women (hirsutism, acne, deepening of voice, clitoromegaly, menstrual irregularities — irreversible voice change), in children premature epiphyseal closure (short stature) and precocious puberty, in men — suppression of spermatogenesis and testicular atrophy (exogenous testosterone is contraceptive — infertility), gynaecomastia (aromatisation), priapism, acne, oily skin, male pattern baldness, polycythaemia (monitor haematocrit), worsening of sleep apnoea, prostate enlargement (BPH symptoms) and possible stimulation of prostate cancer (monitor PSA), dyslipidaemia (↓ HDL), fluid retention, hypertension, cardiovascular risk; cholestatic jaundice, hepatic adenoma, peliosis hepatis and hepatocellular carcinoma with 17α-alkylated derivatives; psychological — aggression, mood swings; abuse by athletes and bodybuilders (anabolic steroid misuse — prohibited by WADA); contraindications — prostate or male breast cancer, pregnancy, polycythaemia, untreated severe sleep apnoea, uncontrolled heart failure. Hormone antagonists and modulators: selective oestrogen receptor modulators — tamoxifen (antagonist in breast, agonist in endometrium/bone — breast cancer treatment and prevention; endometrial cancer, VTE, hot flushes), raloxifene (agonist in bone, antagonist in breast and endometrium — postmenopausal osteoporosis, breast cancer risk reduction; VTE, hot flushes), clomiphene citrate (antagonist at hypothalamus — increases GnRH/FSH — ovulation induction in anovulatory infertility, PCOS; multiple pregnancy, ovarian hyperstimulation), ormeloxifene (centchroman — once-weekly non-hormonal contraceptive 'Chhaya'), bazedoxifene; selective oestrogen receptor degrader — fulvestrant; aromatase inhibitors — letrozole, anastrozole, exemestane (breast cancer in postmenopausal women; ovulation induction — letrozole preferred in PCOS); antiprogestins — mifepristone (medical abortion, emergency contraception, Cushing syndrome), ulipristal acetate (emergency contraception, fibroids); antiandrogens — androgen receptor blockers (flutamide, bicalutamide, enzalutamide, apalutamide — prostate cancer), cyproterone acetate (hirsutism, acne in OCP, precocious puberty, prostate cancer), spironolactone (hirsutism); androgen synthesis inhibitors — abiraterone (CYP17), ketoconazole; 5α-reductase inhibitors — finasteride, dutasteride (BPH, male pattern baldness); GnRH analogues (medical castration).",
[["Sex steroids: uses and ADR",[["Hormone","Main uses","Key ADR"],["Oestrogens","Contraception, HRT, hypogonadism, atrophic vaginitis","VTE, endometrial/breast cancer, gallstones, hypertension, nausea"],["Progestins","Contraception (POP, DMPA, LNG-IUS, EC), HRT, DUB, endometriosis, preterm birth prevention","Irregular bleeding, weight gain, mood change, ↓ BMD (DMPA)"],["Androgens","Male hypogonadism, delayed puberty, anaemia, cachexia, hereditary angioedema","Virilisation, polycythaemia, infertility, hepatotoxicity (17α-alkyl), acne"]]],
["Modulators and antagonists",[["Drug","Action","Use"],["Tamoxifen","SERM (breast antagonist)","Breast cancer"],["Raloxifene","SERM (bone agonist)","Osteoporosis"],["Clomiphene, letrozole","Hypothalamic antagonist / aromatase inhibitor","Ovulation induction"],["Ormeloxifene","SERM","Weekly contraceptive (Chhaya)"],["Mifepristone, ulipristal","Antiprogestin","Medical abortion, EC"],["Flutamide, bicalutamide, enzalutamide","AR antagonist","Prostate cancer"],["Cyproterone, spironolactone","Antiandrogen","Hirsutism, acne"],["Finasteride, dutasteride","5α-reductase inhibitor","BPH, alopecia"]]],
["Contraindications to oestrogen",["Breast/endometrial cancer","Undiagnosed vaginal bleeding","VTE, thrombophilia","Active liver disease","Stroke, MI","Pregnancy","Smokers > 35 years (OCP)"]]],
{i:"Sex steroids and their modulators are used across contraception, menopause, reproductive disorders, hypogonadism and hormone-dependent cancers.",f:"Hypothalamus (GnRH) → pituitary (FSH, LH) → ovary/testis\n Ovary: oestrogen (proliferative endometrium) + progesterone (secretory)\n Testis: testosterone → (5α-reductase) DHT / (aromatase) estradiol\n Negative feedback ← exogenous steroids (basis of contraception)",c:"Oestrogens and progestins are central to contraception and menopausal therapy, androgens treat hypogonadism, and receptor modulators and synthesis inhibitors extend their use to infertility and hormone-dependent cancers — always weighing thrombotic, neoplastic and virilising risks."});

Z("pharm14",6,"ph",["Calcium, vitamin D, parathyroid hormone analogues (teriparatide) and calcitonin regulate calcium and bone metabolism; they are used to treat hypocalcaemia, rickets/osteomalacia, osteoporosis, Paget's disease and hypercalcaemia.","Vitamin D and PTH raise blood calcium (absorb from gut, release from bone, save in kidney); calcitonin lowers it; intermittent teriparatide paradoxically builds bone.",["Calcium salts: carbonate (40% Ca, needs acid), citrate (21%, better with PPIs), gluconate (IV 10% for hypocalcaemic tetany, hyperkalaemia, CCB/Mg toxicity); uses — osteoporosis (with vit D), hypocalcaemia, rickets; ADR — constipation, stones, hypercalcaemia.","Vitamin D: cholecalciferol (D3), ergocalciferol (D2), calcitriol (1,25-(OH)2D — active; renal failure, hypoparathyroidism), alfacalcidol (1α — needs hepatic 25-hydroxylation only); ↑ gut Ca/P absorption; uses — rickets, osteomalacia, vitamin D deficiency (60,000 IU weekly × 8), osteoporosis, hypoparathyroidism, CKD-MBD; toxicity — hypercalcaemia, nephrocalcinosis.","Teriparatide (PTH 1-34): intermittent daily SC → anabolic (↑ osteoblast) — severe osteoporosis, ≤ 2 years; ADR — hypercalcaemia, orthostatic hypotension, osteosarcoma (rats); abaloparatide; continuous PTH → resorption.","Calcitonin (salmon): ↓ osteoclast resorption, ↑ renal Ca excretion; uses — hypercalcaemia (rapid, tachyphylaxis), Paget's disease, acute osteoporotic fracture pain; ADR — nausea, flushing, hypocalcaemia, malignancy concern (nasal). Others: bisphosphonates, denosumab, cinacalcet, romosozumab."],"Teriparatide: intermittent PTH builds bone; continuous PTH breaks bone."],
"Calcium homeostasis (serum calcium 8.5–10.5 mg/dL; 50% ionised) is maintained by parathyroid hormone (PTH), vitamin D (calcitriol) and calcitonin acting on gut, bone and kidney, with FGF23 regulating phosphate. Calcium: preparations — oral: calcium carbonate (40% elemental; cheapest; requires gastric acid — take with meals; also antacid), calcium citrate (21%; absorbed without acid — preferred with PPIs, achlorhydria, elderly), calcium lactate (13%), calcium gluconate (9%), calcium phosphate, calcium dobesilate; parenteral: calcium gluconate 10% (93 mg elemental/10 mL; IV slowly — preferred, less irritant), calcium chloride 10% (273 mg/10 mL; central line, cardiac arrest — irritant); daily requirement 1000–1200 mg; uses — (1) acute hypocalcaemic tetany (post-thyroidectomy/parathyroidectomy, hypoparathyroidism, vitamin D deficiency, pancreatitis, massive transfusion) — 10–20 mL 10% calcium gluconate IV over 10 minutes followed by infusion; (2) hyperkalaemia (cardiac membrane stabilisation), (3) hypermagnesaemia and magnesium sulphate toxicity, (4) calcium channel blocker or β-blocker overdose, (5) black widow spider bite, fluoride and hydrofluoric acid poisoning; (6) chronic — dietary supplementation in osteoporosis (with vitamin D), pregnancy and lactation (prevent pre-eclampsia — 1.5–2 g/day in low-intake populations), rickets and osteomalacia, chronic hypoparathyroidism, phosphate binder in CKD (carbonate, acetate); adverse effects — constipation, flatulence, dyspepsia, hypercalcaemia (with vitamin D), kidney stones, milk-alkali syndrome (high carbonate intake), possible cardiovascular events with supplements; IV — extravasation necrosis, bradycardia, arrhythmias with digoxin (avoid rapid injection in digitalised patients), vasodilatation; interactions — reduces absorption of tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, iron, levothyroxine, bisphosphonates (separate dosing). Vitamin D: cholecalciferol (D3; synthesised in skin by UVB from 7-dehydrocholesterol; fish oils, fortified foods) and ergocalciferol (D2; plant/fungal); hydroxylated in liver to 25-hydroxyvitamin D (calcidiol; main circulating form, measured to assess status; deficiency < 20 ng/mL) and in kidney by 1α-hydroxylase (stimulated by PTH, low phosphate; inhibited by FGF23, calcitriol) to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol; active hormone); acts on nuclear vitamin D receptor — increases intestinal absorption of calcium (calbindin, TRPV6) and phosphate, promotes bone mineralisation (provides Ca and P) and, at high levels, bone resorption via RANKL, increases renal tubular reabsorption of calcium, suppresses PTH synthesis, immunomodulatory and cell differentiation effects; preparations — cholecalciferol (oral 1000–2000 IU daily; 60,000 IU sachet weekly for 8 weeks then monthly — loading in deficiency; 3–6 lakh IU single IM/oral 'stoss' therapy in rickets), ergocalciferol, calcifediol (25-OH D3), calcitriol (0.25–1 µg/day; active form — rapid onset and offset; for CKD and hypoparathyroidism, vitamin D-dependent rickets type 1), alfacalcidol (1α-hydroxycholecalciferol; requires only hepatic 25-hydroxylation — renal failure, hypoparathyroidism), paricalcitol and doxercalciferol (less calcaemic analogues — secondary hyperparathyroidism in CKD), calcipotriol (topical — psoriasis); uses — (1) nutritional rickets (children) and osteomalacia (adults) — prevention (400 IU/day in infants) and treatment; (2) vitamin D deficiency/insufficiency (very common in India); (3) osteoporosis (with calcium; adjunct to bisphosphonates/teriparatide); (4) hypoparathyroidism and pseudohypoparathyroidism (calcitriol/alfacalcidol with calcium); (5) renal osteodystrophy/CKD–mineral bone disorder (calcitriol, alfacalcidol, paricalcitol); (6) vitamin D-dependent rickets (type 1 — calcitriol; type 2 — high doses), hypophosphataemic (vitamin D-resistant) rickets (calcitriol + phosphate; burosumab — anti-FGF23); (7) malabsorption, anticonvulsant-induced osteomalacia, chronic liver disease; (8) psoriasis (topical calcipotriol), (9) Fanconi syndrome; adverse effects — hypervitaminosis D (excess supplements): hypercalcaemia (fatigue, weakness, nausea, vomiting, constipation, polyuria, polydipsia, confusion), hypercalciuria, nephrolithiasis, nephrocalcinosis, renal failure, metastatic calcification, hypertension, arrhythmias; treatment — stop vitamin D, hydration, glucocorticoids, bisphosphonates; calcitriol has short action — toxicity resolves quickly. Parathyroid hormone and teriparatide: PTH (84 amino acids) raises serum calcium by increasing bone resorption (via osteoblast RANKL → osteoclast activation), renal calcium reabsorption (distal tubule), renal 1α-hydroxylation of vitamin D (indirect gut absorption) and phosphate excretion; continuous high PTH (hyperparathyroidism) causes net bone loss, whereas intermittent once-daily exposure stimulates osteoblast differentiation and activity (reduced apoptosis, sclerostin inhibition) more than resorption — net anabolic effect ('anabolic window'); teriparatide (recombinant human PTH 1–34): 20 µg SC once daily for up to 24 months; increases bone mineral density (especially spine) and reduces vertebral (65%) and non-vertebral fractures; indications — severe osteoporosis with high fracture risk (postmenopausal women, men, glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis), multiple vertebral fractures, failure or intolerance of antiresorptive therapy; followed by an antiresorptive (bisphosphonate/denosumab) to maintain gains; recombinant PTH 1–84 (hypoparathyroidism), palopegteriparatide (hypoparathyroidism), abaloparatide (PTHrP analogue); adverse effects — transient hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria, orthostatic hypotension, dizziness, nausea, leg cramps, headache, injection-site reactions, raised uric acid; osteosarcoma in rats at high doses (limited duration historically; caution) — contraindicated in Paget disease, unexplained raised alkaline phosphatase, open epiphyses, prior skeletal radiation, bone metastases, hypercalcaemia, hyperparathyroidism. Calcitonin: 32-amino acid peptide from thyroid parafollicular C cells; salmon calcitonin is 20–40 times more potent and longer acting than human; mechanism — binds calcitonin receptors on osteoclasts (Gs → cAMP), rapidly inhibiting osteoclast activity and bone resorption; increases renal excretion of calcium, phosphate and sodium; analgesic effect (central — endorphins); lowers serum calcium within hours; tachyphylaxis (receptor downregulation) within 2–3 days limits use in hypercalcaemia; routes — SC/IM (4–8 IU/kg 6–12-hourly in hypercalcaemia), intranasal spray (200 IU daily; osteoporosis — now restricted); uses — (1) hypercalcaemia (especially of malignancy) — rapid but modest and short-lived effect; given with saline hydration while waiting for bisphosphonates/denosumab to act; (2) Paget disease of bone (when bisphosphonates contraindicated) — reduces bone turnover, pain; (3) acute pain of recent osteoporotic vertebral fractures (short-term); (4) postmenopausal osteoporosis (weak; intranasal largely withdrawn in Europe due to cancer signal); (5) prevention of bone loss with immobilisation, reflex sympathetic dystrophy (historical); (6) marker of medullary thyroid carcinoma (diagnostic, not therapeutic); adverse effects — nausea, vomiting, facial flushing, metallic taste, local inflammation at injection site, rhinitis and epistaxis (nasal), hypocalcaemia, hypersensitivity, antibody formation (salmon — resistance), possible slight increase in malignancy with long-term use. Other bone and calcium drugs: bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, ibandronate, zoledronic acid — osteoporosis, Paget, hypercalcaemia of malignancy, bone metastases), denosumab (anti-RANKL monoclonal antibody — osteoporosis, bone metastases, hypercalcaemia), romosozumab (anti-sclerostin — anabolic + antiresorptive), raloxifene, strontium ranelate (withdrawn in many countries), cinacalcet and etelcalcetide (calcimimetics — secondary hyperparathyroidism in CKD, parathyroid carcinoma), gallium nitrate, plicamycin (historical), glucocorticoids (vitamin D toxicity, granulomatous disease, myeloma hypercalcaemia), phosphate binders (sevelamer, lanthanum), fluoride.",
[["Comparison",[["Agent","Effect on serum Ca²⁺","Mechanism","Main uses"],["Calcium salts","↑","Supplement","Hypocalcaemia, osteoporosis, hyperkalaemia (IV)"],["Vitamin D / calcitriol","↑","↑ Gut absorption, mineralisation","Rickets, osteomalacia, hypoparathyroidism, CKD-MBD"],["Teriparatide (intermittent PTH)","Slight ↑","Anabolic — ↑ osteoblasts","Severe osteoporosis"],["Calcitonin","↓","↓ Osteoclast resorption, ↑ Ca excretion","Hypercalcaemia, Paget's, fracture pain"],["Bisphosphonates","↓","Osteoclast apoptosis","Osteoporosis, Paget's, hypercalcaemia of malignancy"],["Denosumab","↓","Anti-RANKL","Osteoporosis, metastases"],["Cinacalcet","↓","Calcimimetic (↓ PTH)","Secondary hyperparathyroidism"]]],
["Vitamin D preparations",[["Preparation","Activation needed","Use"],["Cholecalciferol (D3)/ergocalciferol (D2)","Liver + kidney","Deficiency, rickets, osteoporosis"],["Calcifediol (25-OH D)","Kidney","Liver disease"],["Alfacalcidol (1α-OH D)","Liver only","CKD, hypoparathyroidism"],["Calcitriol (1,25-(OH)2 D)","None","CKD, hypoparathyroidism, VDDR type 1"],["Paricalcitol","None","Secondary HPT in CKD"]]],
["Calcium preparations",[["Salt","Elemental Ca","Note"],["Carbonate","40%","Needs acid; take with meals"],["Citrate","21%","Absorbed without acid; stones less"],["Gluconate (IV 10%)","9%","Tetany, hyperkalaemia"],["Chloride (IV 10%)","27%","Central line; cardiac arrest"]]]],
{i:"Bone and calcium disorders are managed by correcting deficiencies (calcium, vitamin D), reducing resorption (bisphosphonates, denosumab, calcitonin) or stimulating formation (teriparatide).",f:"↓ Ca²⁺ → PTH ↑ → bone resorption + renal Ca reabsorption + 1α-hydroxylase → calcitriol → gut Ca absorption → ↑ Ca²⁺\n ↑ Ca²⁺ → calcitonin → ↓ osteoclast activity\n Intermittent teriparatide → osteoblast stimulation (anabolic)",c:"Calcium and vitamin D correct deficiency states and support osteoporosis therapy, calcitriol and alfacalcidol bypass renal activation in CKD, intermittent teriparatide is the main anabolic osteoporosis drug, and calcitonin provides rapid but transient control of hypercalcaemia."});

Z("pharm15",0,"ph",["New drug development is a long, regulated process from discovery and preclinical testing (in vitro and animal studies) through four phases of clinical trials in humans, regulatory approval (CDSCO/DCGI in India), and post-marketing surveillance (phase IV).","It's a funnel: thousands of molecules screened, a few reach animals, fewer reach humans; each phase asks a bigger question — is it safe (I), does it work (II), is it better than current treatment (III), what happens in the real world (IV).",["Discovery: target identification, lead compound (screening, rational design, biotechnology), lead optimisation; preclinical — pharmacology, PK, acute/subacute/chronic toxicity, reproductive, mutagenicity, carcinogenicity (GLP), in 2 species.","Phase I (first-in-human, 20–100 healthy volunteers; patients for anticancer): safety, tolerability, PK, MTD; open-label; microdosing = phase 0.","Phase II (100–500 patients): efficacy, dose-finding (IIa pilot, IIb dose-ranging), short-term safety; Phase III (1000–5000 patients, multicentric, randomised double-blind controlled): confirm efficacy vs placebo/standard, ADRs — basis for NDA/marketing authorisation.","Phase IV (post-marketing surveillance): rare/long-term ADRs, new indications, special populations, pharmacovigilance (PvPI). Ethics: ICH-GCP, Declaration of Helsinki, Ethics Committee approval, informed consent, CTRI registration; New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules 2019; total 10–15 years, ~USD 1–2 billion."],"Phases: I = safety (volunteers), II = efficacy/dose, III = confirm vs standard, IV = post-marketing."],
"New drug development is the process of bringing a new chemical entity or biological to the market after demonstrating quality, safety and efficacy; it takes 10–15 years and over a billion dollars, with only about 1 in 5,000–10,000 screened compounds reaching approval. Stages: (A) Drug discovery — target identification and validation (receptors, enzymes, ion channels, genes; genomics, proteomics), lead identification (random/high-throughput screening of compound libraries, natural products, rational structure-based drug design, computer-aided design, combinatorial chemistry, biotechnology — monoclonal antibodies, recombinant proteins, gene and cell therapies, AI-driven discovery, drug repurposing), lead optimisation (modifying structure for potency, selectivity, pharmacokinetics, safety). (B) Preclinical studies (in vitro and in vivo animal studies, conducted under Good Laboratory Practice) — (1) pharmacological screening: pharmacodynamics (mechanism, dose–response, selectivity), safety pharmacology (CNS, cardiovascular — hERG/QT, respiratory); (2) pharmacokinetics (ADME) in animals; (3) toxicity studies — acute (single-dose; LD50/maximum tolerated dose, in two species), subacute/subchronic (repeated dose 2–4 weeks to 90 days), chronic (6–12 months; depending on intended duration of human use), reproductive and developmental toxicity (fertility, teratogenicity — segment I, II, III), genotoxicity/mutagenicity (Ames test, chromosomal aberration, micronucleus), carcinogenicity (lifetime studies in rodents for drugs used long term), local toxicity, immunotoxicity, allergenicity; animal ethics (CPCSEA approval, 3Rs — replace, reduce, refine). (C) Investigational New Drug application — submitted to regulatory authority (CDSCO — Central Drugs Standard Control Organisation, headed by DCGI in India; FDA in USA) with preclinical data and clinical trial protocol for permission to test in humans; approval by institutional ethics committee and registration in Clinical Trials Registry of India (CTRI). (D) Clinical trials (conducted according to ICH-Good Clinical Practice, Declaration of Helsinki, ICMR National Ethical Guidelines 2017, New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules 2019, with written informed consent (audio-video recording for vulnerable groups in India), compensation for trial-related injury, data safety monitoring boards): Phase 0 (exploratory, microdosing) — sub-therapeutic doses (≤ 100 µg) in 10–15 subjects to obtain early human PK/PD data; Phase I (human pharmacology; first-in-human) — 20–100 healthy volunteers (patients for cytotoxic or highly toxic drugs such as anticancer, anti-HIV); open-label or single-blind; single ascending and multiple ascending doses; aims — safety, tolerability, maximum tolerated dose, pharmacokinetics (ADME, bioavailability, half-life), pharmacodynamics, food and drug interactions; conducted by clinical pharmacologists in specialised units; duration months; Phase II (therapeutic exploration) — 100–500 patients with the target disease; randomised, controlled (often double-blind); phase IIa — proof of concept/pilot efficacy; phase IIb — dose-ranging (optimal dose and regimen), short-term adverse effects; largest attrition (~70% fail); Phase III (therapeutic confirmation) — 1,000–5,000 (or more) patients in multicentric (often multinational) randomised, double-blind, controlled trials comparing with placebo or standard therapy; confirms efficacy and safety in larger and more diverse populations, establishes benefit–risk, detects less common ADRs, drug interactions, special populations; duration 1–4 years; data submitted in New Drug Application (NDA)/Marketing Authorisation Application; regulatory review and approval (with prescribing information, labelling; India may require local phase III data or waive for drugs approved elsewhere); Phase IV (post-marketing surveillance/studies) — after approval and marketing; in thousands to millions of patients under real-world conditions; aims — detect rare, delayed and long-term adverse effects (e.g., withdrawal of rofecoxib, troglitazone, cerivastatin), drug interactions, efficacy in special groups (children, elderly, pregnant women), new indications, comparative effectiveness, pharmacoeconomics, compliance; methods — spontaneous ADR reporting (Pharmacovigilance Programme of India — PvPI, at IPC Ghaziabad; Suspected ADR reporting form; Uppsala Monitoring Centre), cohort and case–control studies, registries, Periodic Safety Update Reports (PSURs every 6 months for 2 years, then annually); no fixed duration. Trial design concepts: randomisation (avoid selection bias), blinding (single, double, triple — avoid observer/subject bias), placebo control (ethical when no proven therapy), active control (non-inferiority/equivalence/superiority), crossover and parallel designs, intention-to-treat analysis, sample size and power, primary and secondary endpoints, surrogate endpoints, adaptive designs. Other regulatory pathways: orphan drugs, fast-track/accelerated approval, compassionate use, emergency use authorisation (COVID-19 vaccines), biosimilars, generics (bioequivalence studies — 80–125% confidence limits for AUC and Cmax).",
[["Phases of clinical trials",[["Phase","Subjects","Number","Purpose"],["0","Healthy volunteers","10–15","Microdosing; early PK/PD"],["I","Healthy volunteers (patients for anticancer)","20–100","Safety, tolerability, PK, MTD"],["II","Patients","100–500","Efficacy (proof of concept), dose-finding, short-term safety"],["III","Patients (multicentric RCT)","1,000–5,000+","Confirm efficacy vs placebo/standard; ADRs; basis of approval"],["IV","Patients (post-marketing)","Thousands–millions","Rare/long-term ADRs, new indications, special populations"]]],
["Preclinical studies",[["Study","Details"],["Pharmacodynamics","Mechanism, dose–response, selectivity"],["Safety pharmacology","CNS, CVS (hERG/QT), respiratory"],["Pharmacokinetics","ADME in animals"],["Acute toxicity","Single dose, 2 species"],["Subacute/chronic toxicity","Repeated dose, 2 weeks–12 months"],["Reproductive toxicity","Fertility, teratogenicity"],["Genotoxicity","Ames test, chromosomal aberration, micronucleus"],["Carcinogenicity","Lifetime rodent studies"]]],
["Ethical and regulatory framework",["ICH-GCP, Declaration of Helsinki","ICMR National Ethical Guidelines 2017","New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules 2019 (CDSCO/DCGI)","Ethics Committee approval, informed consent (AV recording for vulnerable)","CTRI registration","Compensation for trial injury","Pharmacovigilance (PvPI)"]]],
{i:"Drug development is a high-risk, high-cost process designed to ensure that new medicines are safe and effective before and after reaching patients.",f:"Target → lead → optimisation → preclinical (GLP, animals) → IND/CT permission + EC approval + CTRI\n → Phase I (safety, PK) → Phase II (efficacy, dose) → Phase III (confirm, RCT) → NDA → approval (CDSCO)\n → Phase IV (pharmacovigilance, PSUR)",c:"New drug development proceeds from discovery through preclinical toxicology and phased clinical trials to approval and post-marketing surveillance, all under ethical and regulatory safeguards that protect participants and patients."});

Z("pharm15",1,"ph",["Essential medicines are those that satisfy the priority health-care needs of the population and should be available at all times in adequate amounts, appropriate dosage forms, assured quality and affordable price; India's National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM 2022) has 384 medicines. A P-drug (personal drug) is a doctor's own preferred, rationally chosen drug for a common condition.","The essential medicines list is the 'core toolkit' every health system must stock; the P-drug is the specific tool a doctor has personally chosen, tested and knows well.",["Concept: WHO Model List (1977; updated every 2 years; ~500 medicines; core and complementary lists); India NLEM 1996, 2003, 2011, 2015, 2022 (384 drugs, 27 categories) — basis of price control (DPCO/NPPA), procurement, free drug schemes.","Selection criteria: public health relevance (disease prevalence), proven efficacy and safety, comparative cost-effectiveness, availability, stability, single compounds preferred (FDCs only if proven advantage), generic names, levels of care (P, S, T).","Benefits: rational prescribing, better supply/procurement, lower cost, training, quality assurance, health equity; Jan Aushadhi generic stores.","P-drug (WHO Guide to Good Prescribing): define diagnosis → specify therapeutic objective → list effective drug groups → choose group by efficacy, safety, suitability, cost → choose P-drug (active substance, dosage form, schedule, duration) → verify suitability for individual patient."],"Essential medicine criteria: 'Efficacy, Safety, Suitability, Cost' (and relevance)."],
"Essential medicines concept (WHO, 1977): 'Essential medicines are those that satisfy the priority health care needs of the population. They are selected with due regard to public health relevance, evidence on efficacy and safety, and comparative cost-effectiveness. Essential medicines are intended to be available within the context of functioning health systems at all times in adequate amounts, in the appropriate dosage forms, with assured quality and adequate information, and at a price the individual and the community can afford.' The WHO Model List of Essential Medicines is revised every two years (23rd list 2023, ~500 medicines) with a core list (minimum needs for a basic health system — most efficacious, safe and cost-effective) and a complementary list (for priority diseases needing specialised facilities or training), plus a separate list for children (since 2007) and the AWaRe classification of antibiotics (Access, Watch, Reserve). National List of Essential Medicines (India): first prepared in 1996, revised 2003, 2011, 2015 and 2022 (by the Standing National Committee on Medicines under the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare); NLEM 2022 contains 384 medicines across 27 therapeutic categories (34 added, 26 deleted), categorised by level of health care — primary (P), secondary (S) and tertiary (T); includes medicines such as ivermectin, mupirocin, insulin glargine, teneligliptin, bedaquiline, delamanid, dolutegravir, rotavirus vaccine; deletions include ranitidine, sucralfate, atenolol, nicotinamide; NLEM is used for price control (medicines in NLEM are scheduled under the Drug Price Control Order — ceiling prices fixed by National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority), public procurement, free drug distribution in government facilities (Free Drugs Service Initiative), State Essential Medicines Lists, Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana (generic medicine outlets), undergraduate teaching and standard treatment guidelines. Criteria for selection (NLEM): (1) approved and licensed in India; (2) useful in disease that is a public health problem in India; (3) proven efficacy and safety based on scientific evidence; (4) comparative cost-effectiveness (total cost of treatment, not unit cost); (5) aligned with current treatment guidelines; (6) stable under storage conditions, (7) when two or more medicines are equally effective, preference to the one with best safety, efficacy, cost, availability and pharmacokinetic properties; (8) fixed-dose combinations included only when they have proven advantages over single compounds in therapeutic effect, safety, compliance or reducing resistance (e.g., antitubercular FDCs, ACT, ORS, cotrimoxazole, ART); (9) medicines listed by generic (INN) names; (10) price sensitivity; deletion if banned, safety concerns, better alternative available, or resistance. Advantages of the essential medicines concept: improves availability and access, rational use and prescribing (limited list easier to know well), economy in procurement, storage, distribution (bulk purchase, lower prices), better quality assurance and drug information, training of health workers, reduced irrational FDCs, basis for insurance reimbursement and standard treatment guidelines, health equity — progress towards Universal Health Coverage. Limitations: may restrict physician choice; needs regular updating; does not guarantee availability or quality. P-drug concept (WHO Guide to Good Prescribing, 1994): P-drugs (personal drugs) are the drugs a prescriber has chosen to prescribe regularly for priority conditions, with which they have become familiar — a personal formulary (usually 40–60 drugs); prescribing from a limited, carefully selected set avoids repeated searching, enhances knowledge of ADRs, interactions and dosing, and promotes rational, economical prescribing; P-drug selection is done in advance (not for a particular patient) and then applied: steps — (1) define the diagnosis (pathophysiology); (2) specify the therapeutic objective; (3) make an inventory of effective drug groups (by mechanism); (4) choose an effective group according to criteria — efficacy, safety, suitability (contraindications, interactions, dosage form, schedule, patient groups) and cost (comparative table); (5) choose a P-drug from the group — active substance and dosage form, standard dosage schedule and standard duration of treatment; then for an individual patient — verify that the P-drug is suitable (efficacy, contraindications, interactions, convenience), modify if necessary; write a prescription, give information, instructions and warnings, monitor (and stop) treatment — the 'six-step process of rational treatment'. Example: P-drug for uncomplicated hypertension — amlodipine 5 mg once daily; P-drug for community-acquired pneumonia — amoxicillin.",
[["NLEM at a glance",[["Feature","Detail"],["First list","1996 (WHO: 1977)"],["Latest","NLEM 2022 — 384 medicines, 27 categories"],["Levels","Primary (P), secondary (S), tertiary (T)"],["Uses","Price control (DPCO/NPPA), procurement, free drugs, Jan Aushadhi, teaching"],["Names","Generic (INN)"]]],
["Selection criteria",["Public health relevance","Proven efficacy and safety","Comparative cost-effectiveness","Stability, availability","Single drugs preferred; FDCs only if proven advantage","Licensed in India, in treatment guidelines"]],
["P-drug selection steps",[["Step","Action"],["1","Define diagnosis"],["2","Specify therapeutic objective"],["3","List effective drug groups"],["4","Choose group: efficacy, safety, suitability, cost"],["5","Choose P-drug: substance, dosage form, schedule, duration"],["6","Verify suitability for the individual patient"]]]],
{c:"The essential medicines concept, embodied in WHO's model list and India's NLEM 2022, ensures access to cost-effective, safe drugs, while the P-drug approach helps individual doctors prescribe rationally from a familiar personal formulary."});

Z("pharm15",2,"ph",["A prescription is a written order by a registered medical practitioner to a pharmacist to dispense specified drugs for a patient, with directions for use; rational prescribing means giving the right drug, in the right dose, for the right duration, at the lowest cost, to the right patient.","A good prescription is clear, complete, legible, in generic names, and readable by anyone — no guessing, no abbreviations that can be misread.",["Parts: superscription (date, patient's name, age, sex, address, weight; symbol 'Rx' — 'take thou'), inscription (drug names — generic, capital letters, strength, dosage form), subscription (instructions to pharmacist — quantity), transcription/signa (directions to patient — dose, frequency, route, timing, duration), prescriber's name, signature, registration number, address.","Good practice (MCI/NMC): generic names, legible capital letters, avoid abbreviations (U for units, µg), no trailing zeros (5 mg not 5.0), leading zero (0.5), total quantity in words for controlled drugs, sign every page.","Rational use (WHO): appropriate drug for clinical need, dose, duration, at lowest cost; irrational practices — polypharmacy, unnecessary antibiotics/injections, irrational FDCs, inadequate dose, brand prescribing, failure to follow STGs.","Promoting rational prescribing: essential medicines list, STGs, P-drug, antimicrobial stewardship, drug and therapeutics committees, prescription audit, continuing education, independent drug information, patient education."],"Prescription parts: 'Superscription, Inscription, Subscription, Signa'."],
"A prescription is a written (or electronic) order from a registered medical practitioner (or other authorised prescriber) to a pharmacist to prepare and dispense medicines for a specified patient and instructing the patient on their use; it is a medicolegal document. Parts of a prescription: (1) heading/prescriber details — name, qualifications, registration number (State Medical Council/NMC), address, telephone; (2) date; (3) patient details — name, age, sex, weight (essential for children, elderly), address, diagnosis/provisional diagnosis (recommended), allergies; (4) superscription — the symbol 'Rx' (Latin recipe — 'take thou'); (5) inscription — the main part: names of drugs (preferably generic/INN names in capital letters), strength and dosage form (e.g., TAB AMLODIPINE 5 mg); one drug per line; (6) subscription — directions to the pharmacist regarding preparation and the total quantity to be dispensed (number of tablets, volume); (7) transcription (signa — 'mark') — directions to the patient in the language understood: dose, route, frequency, timing in relation to meals, duration of treatment, special instructions (e.g., 'take after food', 'avoid alcohol', 'complete the course'); (8) refill instructions; (9) signature of prescriber (with stamp); (10) follow-up/review date and non-drug advice (diet, lifestyle, warning signs). NMC (MCI Code of Ethics 2016 amendment) requires every physician to prescribe drugs with generic names legibly, preferably in capital letters, and ensure rational prescription and use of drugs. Good prescribing practices: write legibly or use computerised prescriptions; generic names; avoid abbreviations open to error ('U' for units, 'IU', 'QD', 'µg' — write 'micrograms', 'OD'/'BD' acceptable if standard); avoid trailing zeros (write 5 mg, not 5.0 mg) and use leading zeros (0.5 mg, not .5 mg); avoid decimal points where possible (500 micrograms rather than 0.5 mg); specify dose in metric units; write total quantity in words and figures for controlled drugs (NDPS Act, Schedule X — prescription in duplicate, retained by pharmacist); avoid prescribing by brand unless essential (biologics, narrow therapeutic index drugs); state duration; check allergies, interactions, renal/hepatic function, pregnancy and lactation; consider patient's affordability, convenience (fewer doses), dosage form; verbal orders only in emergencies and confirm in writing; sign and date every prescription; keep copies/records. Types of prescriptions: precompounded (manufactured preparations — most common), extemporaneous (compounded by pharmacist — rare now), electronic/e-prescription (telemedicine guidelines 2020). Rational drug use (WHO, Nairobi 1985): 'Patients receive medications appropriate to their clinical needs, in doses that meet their own individual requirements, for an adequate period of time, and at the lowest cost to them and their community.' Components ('5 Rs'/'rights'): right patient, right drug (efficacy, safety, suitability, cost), right dose, right route, right time/duration, right information, right documentation, right monitoring. Irrational prescribing practices: polypharmacy (> 3–5 drugs without need), unnecessary use of antibiotics (viral URTIs, diarrhoea) — antimicrobial resistance, overuse of injections (unsafe injections, cost), irrational fixed-dose combinations (many banned by CDSCO — e.g., nimesulide + paracetamol), tonics, vitamins and 'enzyme' preparations without indication, use of expensive brands/newer drugs when older cheaper ones are equally effective, wrong or inadequate dosing, failure to follow standard treatment guidelines, self-medication and over-the-counter misuse of prescription drugs, prescribing under influence of pharmaceutical promotion, inappropriate prescribing in elderly (Beers criteria) and children; consequences — ineffective treatment, adverse effects, drug resistance, increased cost, loss of patient trust, wastage. Strategies to promote rational use: (1) educational — undergraduate pharmacotherapy training (WHO Guide to Good Prescribing, P-drug), continuing medical education, independent drug bulletins and formularies (National Formulary of India), patient education; (2) managerial — essential medicines lists, standard treatment guidelines (ICMR STGs), hospital drug and therapeutics committees, hospital formularies, prescription audit and feedback, antimicrobial stewardship programmes (ICMR AMSP; restricted 'Reserve' antibiotics), computerised decision support; (3) regulatory — banning irrational FDCs, scheduling of drugs (Schedule H, H1 for antibiotics and anti-TB drugs — record keeping; Schedule X), regulation of drug promotion (UCPMP code), price control (DPCO), generic prescribing mandate, red line campaign on antibiotics; (4) economic — Jan Aushadhi generic stores, free drug schemes, insurance formularies.",
[["Parts of a prescription",[["Part","Content"],["Prescriber details","Name, qualification, registration no., address"],["Date & patient details","Name, age, sex, weight, address, diagnosis, allergies"],["Superscription","Rx ('take thou')"],["Inscription","Drug (generic, capitals), strength, dosage form"],["Subscription","Instructions to pharmacist; quantity"],["Transcription (signa)","Directions to patient: dose, frequency, route, timing, duration"],["Signature","Prescriber's signature and stamp; review date"]]],
["Irrational practices",["Polypharmacy","Unnecessary antibiotics and injections","Irrational FDCs, tonics","Brand/expensive drugs without need","Wrong dose/duration","Ignoring STGs","Self-medication"]],
["Strategies for rational use",[["Type","Measures"],["Educational","P-drug training, CME, independent drug information, patient education"],["Managerial","EML, STGs, DTCs, prescription audit, antimicrobial stewardship"],["Regulatory","Banning irrational FDCs, Schedule H1/X, generic prescribing mandate, red line campaign"],["Economic","Jan Aushadhi, free drug schemes, price control"]]]],
{c:"A complete, legible generic-name prescription is a medicolegal and safety tool; rational prescribing — right drug, dose, duration and cost for the right patient — is promoted by education, essential medicines, guidelines, stewardship and regulation."});

Z("pharm15",3,"ph",["Special populations — pregnant and lactating women, children and the elderly — handle drugs differently and are more vulnerable to harm, so drug choice and doses must be adjusted.","Pregnancy adds a second patient (the fetus); children are not small adults (immature organs); the elderly have ageing organs and many medicines — 'start low, go slow'.",["Pregnancy: placental transfer of lipid-soluble, small, unionised drugs; teratogenic period (organogenesis 3–8 weeks); teratogens — thalidomide, isotretinoin, valproate, phenytoin, warfarin, ACEI/ARB, methotrexate, tetracyclines, lithium, misoprostol; safe — paracetamol, penicillins, cephalosporins, insulin, methyldopa, labetalol, levothyroxine, folic acid; FDA categories → PLLR narrative labelling.","Lactation: most drugs safe; avoid/caution — antineoplastics, lithium, radioactive iodine, amiodarone, ergotamine, chloramphenicol, tetracyclines (prolonged), high-dose metronidazole; take after feeding.","Children: immature absorption, ↑ body water, ↓ protein binding, immature hepatic glucuronidation (gray baby, kernicterus), immature renal excretion, BBB permeability; dose by weight/BSA (Clark's, Young's rules historical); avoid aspirin (Reye's), tetracyclines (teeth), fluoroquinolones (cartilage), codeine.","Elderly: ↓ renal function (GFR — Cockcroft–Gault), ↓ hepatic metabolism, ↑ fat, ↓ albumin, ↑ CNS sensitivity, ↓ baroreflex; polypharmacy, falls, delirium; Beers criteria/STOPP–START; avoid long-acting BZDs, anticholinergics, glibenclamide, NSAIDs."],"Elderly prescribing: 'start low, go slow, review often'."],
"Pregnancy: physiological changes alter pharmacokinetics — delayed gastric emptying, increased plasma volume and body water (larger Vd, lower concentrations), reduced albumin (more free drug), increased GFR (faster renal clearance — lithium, penicillins), induced hepatic enzymes (CYP3A4, 2D6, UGT — lamotrigine, phenytoin levels fall), fetal compartment. Placental transfer: most drugs cross by passive diffusion — favoured by lipid solubility, low molecular weight (< 500 Da), low ionisation and protein binding; large polar molecules (heparin, insulin, succinylcholine) cross poorly; IgG antibodies cross actively (third trimester). Effects on fetus depend on timing: pre-implantation (0–2 weeks after conception) — 'all-or-none' effect (death or intact survival); organogenesis/embryonic period (3–8 weeks) — maximum risk of structural malformations (teratogenesis); fetal period (9 weeks to term) — functional and growth abnormalities, organ toxicity (ACE inhibitors — renal dysgenesis; NSAIDs — premature closure of ductus arteriosus, oligohydramnios; tetracyclines — teeth; aminoglycosides — ototoxicity), neonatal effects near term (opioids — respiratory depression and withdrawal; benzodiazepines — floppy infant; sulfonamides — kernicterus; chloramphenicol — gray baby; aspirin — bleeding; propylthiouracil vs methimazole considerations). Known teratogens: thalidomide (phocomelia), isotretinoin and acitretin (retinoid embryopathy), valproate (neural tube defects, reduced IQ), carbamazepine, phenytoin (fetal hydantoin syndrome), warfarin (nasal hypoplasia, chondrodysplasia punctata), ACE inhibitors and ARBs (2nd/3rd trimester fetopathy), methotrexate and other cytotoxics (aminopterin syndrome), lithium (Ebstein anomaly), diethylstilbestrol (vaginal adenocarcinoma), androgens and danazol (virilisation), tetracyclines, misoprostol (Möbius syndrome), mycophenolate, leflunomide, statins, ribavirin, radioactive iodine, alcohol (fetal alcohol syndrome), methimazole (aplasia cutis — first trimester). Drugs considered safe/preferred: paracetamol (analgesic), penicillins, cephalosporins, erythromycin/azithromycin, metronidazole (after first trimester), nitrofurantoin (not at term), insulin and metformin (diabetes), methyldopa, labetalol, nifedipine (hypertension), levothyroxine, propylthiouracil (first trimester), heparin/LMWH (anticoagulation), folic acid, iron, chloroquine, artemisinins (2nd/3rd), quinine, H1 antihistamines (doxylamine, chlorpheniramine), ranitidine/famotidine, PPIs, inhaled salbutamol and budesonide, prednisolone, magnesium sulphate, lamotrigine/levetiracetam, ondansetron (after first trimester). Risk categorisation: US FDA pregnancy categories A, B, C, D, X (replaced in 2015 by the Pregnancy and Lactation Labelling Rule — narrative risk summary); Australian ADEC categories. Principles: avoid drugs in first trimester unless essential; use oldest, best-studied drug at lowest effective dose for shortest time; prefer monotherapy; consider non-drug measures; counsel women of reproductive age (contraception with teratogens, pre-conception folic acid, AED review); topical or inhaled routes where possible. Lactation: most drugs pass into milk by passive diffusion (favoured by lipid solubility, low protein binding, weak bases — ion trapping in slightly acidic milk, low molecular weight); infant dose usually < 10% of maternal weight-adjusted dose (relative infant dose) — compatible; drugs contraindicated or to be used with great caution: anticancer drugs, immunosuppressants (cyclophosphamide), radiopharmaceuticals (interrupt feeding), lithium, amiodarone, iodides, ergotamine and bromocriptine/cabergoline (suppress lactation), chloramphenicol, tetracyclines (prolonged), fluoroquinolones (caution), high-dose metronidazole (interrupt 12–24 h after single 2 g), estrogens (reduce milk; combined OCP avoided first 6 weeks), sulfonamides (neonatal jaundice, G6PD), codeine/tramadol (ultra-rapid metabolisers — infant opioid toxicity), benzodiazepines (sedation), drugs of abuse; precautions — prefer short-acting drugs with high protein binding, take the dose immediately after a feed or before the infant's longest sleep, monitor infant for sedation, rash, diarrhoea; consult LactMed. Children (paediatric pharmacology): pharmacokinetic differences — absorption: neonatal gastric pH higher (better absorption of penicillin; less of weak acids), irregular gastric emptying, thin skin with large surface area (topical toxicity — hexachlorophene, steroids, boric acid); IM absorption erratic; distribution — higher total body water and ECF (higher doses of water-soluble drugs like gentamicin per kg), less fat, lower albumin and binding (bilirubin displacement — kernicterus with sulfonamides, ceftriaxone), immature BBB (more CNS effects — opioids, kernicterus); metabolism — immature CYP and glucuronidation at birth (gray baby syndrome with chloramphenicol, prolonged t½ of caffeine, diazepam), maturing over months; children 1–10 years often metabolise faster than adults (higher mg/kg doses of theophylline, phenytoin, carbamazepine); excretion — GFR and tubular secretion low in neonates (adult values by 6–12 months) — longer dosing intervals for aminoglycosides, vancomycin, penicillins; pharmacodynamic differences — paradoxical excitement with sedatives/antihistamines, growth effects (glucocorticoids — growth suppression, tetracyclines — teeth, fluoroquinolones — cartilage), Reye syndrome (aspirin in viral illness), codeine contraindicated < 12 years, increased sensitivity to some drugs (local anaesthetics, digoxin less sensitive). Dose calculation: by body weight (mg/kg — most common) or body surface area (mg/m² — anticancer drugs; BSA formula: child dose = adult dose × BSA/1.73 m²); historical rules — Young's rule (age/(age + 12) × adult dose), Clark's rule (weight in lb/150 × adult dose), Dilling's rule; formulations — syrups, drops, dispersible tablets, sugar-free preparations; avoid adult preparations split arbitrarily; off-label use common (paediatric trials limited); compliance aided by palatable formulations and caregiver education. Elderly (geriatric pharmacology): physiological changes — reduced lean body mass and total body water, increased body fat (larger Vd and prolonged t½ of lipid-soluble drugs — diazepam; higher concentrations of water-soluble drugs — digoxin, lithium, aminoglycosides), reduced serum albumin (higher free fraction of phenytoin, warfarin), reduced hepatic mass and blood flow (decreased first-pass and phase I metabolism — propranolol, verapamil, benzodiazepines; phase II relatively preserved — prefer lorazepam, oxazepam), reduced renal function (GFR falls ~1 mL/min/year after 40; serum creatinine may be normal due to low muscle mass — estimate by Cockcroft–Gault/eGFR) — dose reduction of renally excreted drugs (digoxin, aminoglycosides, lithium, metformin, DOACs, gabapentin, LMWH); pharmacodynamic changes — increased CNS sensitivity (benzodiazepines, opioids, anticholinergics, antipsychotics — sedation, confusion, delirium, falls), impaired baroreflexes (postural hypotension with antihypertensives, α-blockers, diuretics, TCAs), impaired thermoregulation, increased sensitivity to warfarin, reduced β-receptor responsiveness, impaired homeostasis (hyponatraemia with SSRIs, thiazides; hypoglycaemia with SUs); problems — multimorbidity and polypharmacy (≥ 5 drugs) increasing ADRs (5–10 times more common; leading cause of hospital admissions), drug interactions, prescribing cascades (treating an ADR with another drug), poor compliance (cognitive impairment, visual problems, complex regimens, cost), atypical presentations of ADRs (confusion, falls, incontinence); drugs to avoid/use with caution (Beers criteria, STOPP/START, Fit fOR The Aged): long-acting benzodiazepines (diazepam, chlordiazepoxide), first-generation antihistamines and anticholinergics (diphenhydramine, promethazine, amitriptyline, oxybutynin), glibenclamide and chlorpropamide (prolonged hypoglycaemia), NSAIDs (GI bleeding, renal failure, heart failure), pethidine, digoxin > 0.125 mg, antipsychotics in dementia (stroke, mortality), muscle relaxants, PPIs > 8 weeks without indication, sliding scale insulin, nitrofurantoin with low CrCl; principles — 'start low, go slow', review all medications regularly (deprescribing), use fewest drugs, once-daily regimens, clear written instructions, compliance aids (pill boxes, blister packs), involve caregivers, consider life expectancy and goals of care, monitor renal function and drug levels.",
[["Special populations: key differences",[["Group","PK/PD changes","Key precautions"],["Pregnancy","↑ Vd, ↑ GFR, enzyme induction, placental transfer","Avoid teratogens (1st trimester); use well-studied drugs"],["Lactation","Drugs enter milk (lipid-soluble, weak bases)","Avoid cytotoxics, lithium, amiodarone, radioiodine; dose after feed"],["Children","Immature metabolism/excretion (neonates), ↑ body water, ↓ binding","Dose by weight/BSA; avoid aspirin, tetracyclines, FQ, codeine, chloramphenicol"],["Elderly","↓ GFR, ↓ hepatic metabolism, ↑ fat, ↓ albumin, ↑ CNS sensitivity","Start low, go slow; Beers criteria; deprescribe"]]],
["Teratogens",[["Drug","Defect"],["Thalidomide","Phocomelia"],["Isotretinoin","Retinoid embryopathy"],["Valproate","Neural tube defects"],["Phenytoin","Fetal hydantoin syndrome"],["Warfarin","Nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses"],["ACEI/ARB","Renal dysgenesis, oligohydramnios"],["Lithium","Ebstein anomaly"],["Tetracyclines","Teeth discolouration"],["Methotrexate","Aminopterin syndrome"],["Misoprostol","Möbius syndrome"],["DES","Vaginal adenocarcinoma"]]],
["Drugs to avoid in elderly (Beers)",["Long-acting benzodiazepines","First-generation antihistamines, anticholinergics, TCAs","Glibenclamide, chlorpropamide","NSAIDs (long-term)","Pethidine","Antipsychotics in dementia","High-dose digoxin"]]],
{i:"Most ADRs and therapeutic failures in special populations stem from unrecognised pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic differences.",f:"Pregnancy: 0–2 wk all-or-none → 3–8 wk organogenesis (teratogenesis) → fetal period (growth/function) → term (neonatal effects)\n Children: neonate immature CYP/GFR → toddler faster metabolism → adult\n Elderly: ↓ GFR, ↓ liver, ↑ sensitivity → ↓ dose",c:"Pregnant and lactating women, children and the elderly require careful drug selection and dose adjustment based on altered pharmacokinetics, vulnerability to teratogenic, developmental and age-related toxicity, and evidence-based safety data."});

Z("pharm15",4,"ph",["Therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM) is the measurement of drug concentrations in blood to individualise dosing for drugs with a narrow therapeutic index, large inter-individual variability or difficult-to-measure effects — e.g., digoxin, lithium, phenytoin, aminoglycosides, vancomycin, cyclosporine.","When the gap between the helping dose and the harmful dose is small and patients vary a lot, measuring the blood level is the only reliable way to hit the target.",["Indications: narrow therapeutic index, wide PK variability, poor dose–response correlation, no simple clinical endpoint, suspected toxicity/non-compliance, drug interactions, organ failure, special populations.","Drugs: antiepileptics (phenytoin 10–20 µg/mL, carbamazepine, valproate, phenobarbitone), lithium (0.6–1.2 mEq/L), digoxin (0.5–0.9 ng/mL), aminoglycosides (gentamicin trough < 1), vancomycin (AUC 400–600), theophylline (10–20 µg/mL), cyclosporine, tacrolimus, sirolimus, methotrexate, amiodarone, some antiretrovirals/antifungals (voriconazole).","Not needed: drugs with wide margin or measurable effect (antihypertensives — BP, antidiabetics — glucose, anticoagulants — INR, diuretics), irreversible/hit-and-run drugs, drugs with active metabolites.","Sampling: at steady state (4–5 t½), trough (just before next dose) usually; peak for aminoglycosides; correct timing documented; interpret with clinical picture, free vs total (phenytoin with low albumin)."],"TDM drugs 'Lithium, Digoxin, Phenytoin, Aminoglycosides, Vancomycin, Cyclosporine'."],
"Therapeutic drug monitoring is the measurement and interpretation of drug concentrations in biological fluids (usually plasma/serum, sometimes whole blood or saliva) to optimise individual dosage regimens — maximising efficacy and minimising toxicity. It is based on the premise that plasma concentration correlates better with drug effect than dose does. Indications/criteria for TDM: (1) drugs with a narrow therapeutic index (small margin between effective and toxic concentrations — digoxin, lithium, aminoglycosides, phenytoin); (2) large inter-individual pharmacokinetic variability due to genetics (polymorphic metabolism), age, disease, drug interactions — so the same dose produces widely different levels; (3) non-linear (saturation, zero-order) kinetics where small dose changes produce disproportionate changes in level (phenytoin); (4) absence of an easily measurable clinical or surrogate endpoint (prophylaxis of seizures, mania, transplant rejection, arrhythmias) — unlike BP, blood glucose, INR; (5) established relationship between plasma concentration and therapeutic/toxic effects; (6) to confirm suspected toxicity or poisoning (digoxin, lithium, paracetamol, salicylate, theophylline, iron); (7) to check compliance (non-adherence suspected as cause of failure — antiepileptics, antiretrovirals, immunosuppressants); (8) therapeutic failure despite adequate doses; (9) altered physiology — renal or hepatic failure, pregnancy, elderly, neonates, critically ill (augmented clearance, fluid shifts), obesity, burns, cardiac failure, dialysis; (10) drug interactions (adding/removing enzyme inducers or inhibitors); (11) when changing formulations or brands (generic switch of antiepileptics). Drugs commonly monitored and therapeutic ranges: antiepileptics — phenytoin 10–20 µg/mL (free 1–2), carbamazepine 4–12 µg/mL, valproate 50–100 µg/mL, phenobarbitone 15–40 µg/mL, (lamotrigine, levetiracetam occasionally); mood stabiliser — lithium 0.6–1.0 mEq/L (maintenance), up to 1.2 (acute mania); cardiovascular — digoxin 0.5–0.9 ng/mL (HF), amiodarone 1–2.5 µg/mL, procainamide/NAPA, lignocaine 1.5–5 µg/mL; antimicrobials — gentamicin (peak 5–10 µg/mL conventional; trough < 1 µg/mL; extended-interval nomograms), amikacin (trough < 4–5), vancomycin (AUC/MIC 400–600; trough 10–20 µg/mL historically), voriconazole (trough 1–5.5 µg/mL), flucytosine, some antitubercular drugs and antiretrovirals in special cases; immunosuppressants — cyclosporine (trough 100–400 ng/mL depending on organ and time; C2), tacrolimus (5–15 ng/mL), sirolimus, everolimus, mycophenolate (AUC); bronchodilator — theophylline 10–20 µg/mL (5–15 preferred); anticancer — methotrexate (high-dose — to guide leucovorin rescue), busulfan, 5-FU; others — salicylates (poisoning), paracetamol (poisoning nomogram), tricyclic antidepressants (nortriptyline 50–150 ng/mL), clozapine (> 350 ng/mL). Drugs for which TDM is not useful: drugs with a wide therapeutic index (penicillins); drugs whose effect is easily measured clinically or by a biomarker (antihypertensives — BP, antidiabetics — glucose/HbA1c, warfarin — INR, heparin — aPTT, diuretics, analgesics, lipid-lowering drugs — lipid profile); drugs acting irreversibly or by 'hit and run' mechanism (aspirin on platelets, omeprazole, MAO inhibitors, organophosphates, alkylating agents); drugs whose effects are due mainly to active metabolites not measured (prodrugs); drugs with tolerance development; drugs without established concentration–effect relationship. Practical aspects: timing of sampling — after steady state is achieved (4–5 half-lives after starting or changing dose; earlier if loading dose given), usually trough level (immediately before next dose) for most drugs; peak levels for aminoglycosides (30 min after end of infusion), digoxin sampled at least 6–8 h after dose (distribution phase), lithium 12 h after last dose; record time of last dose, dose history, sample time, co-medications, renal/hepatic function, albumin; interpretation — consider free vs total concentration (phenytoin in hypoalbuminaemia, uraemia, pregnancy — correct or measure free level; Sheiner–Tozer equation), active metabolites (carbamazepine epoxide), patient clinical status (treat the patient, not the level — some patients respond or develop toxicity outside the 'range'), assay method and laboratory reference; dose adjustment — proportional for linear kinetics; cautious small increments for saturable kinetics (phenytoin); Bayesian and model-informed precision dosing software increasingly used (vancomycin AUC). Benefits: individualised therapy, fewer toxic effects (aminoglycoside nephro-/ototoxicity, digoxin toxicity), improved outcomes (transplant rejection prevention), cost savings, detection of non-compliance, management of interactions; limitations: cost, availability, need for correct sampling, therapeutic ranges are population-based guides, turnaround time, misinterpretation. Pharmacogenetic testing (TPMT, HLA-B*1502, CYP2C19, CYP2C9/VKORC1) complements TDM.",
[["Indications for TDM",["Narrow therapeutic index","Large PK variability / genetic polymorphism","Saturation (zero-order) kinetics","No easily measurable endpoint","Suspected toxicity or poisoning","Non-compliance","Organ dysfunction, pregnancy, extremes of age","Drug interactions, formulation switch"]],
["Drugs and therapeutic ranges",[["Drug","Range","Sampling"],["Phenytoin","10–20 µg/mL","Trough; free level if low albumin"],["Carbamazepine","4–12 µg/mL","Trough (autoinduction)"],["Valproate","50–100 µg/mL","Trough"],["Lithium","0.6–1.0 (–1.2) mEq/L","12 h post-dose"],["Digoxin","0.5–0.9 ng/mL","≥ 6–8 h post-dose"],["Gentamicin","Trough < 1 µg/mL; peak 5–10","Trough/peak"],["Vancomycin","AUC 400–600 (trough 10–20)","Trough/AUC"],["Theophylline","10–20 µg/mL","Trough"],["Cyclosporine","100–400 ng/mL (organ-dependent)","Trough / C2"],["Tacrolimus","5–15 ng/mL","Trough"]]],
["TDM not useful",["Wide therapeutic index (penicillins)","Effect measurable (BP, glucose, INR, aPTT)","Irreversible / hit-and-run drugs (aspirin, PPIs, MAOIs)","Prodrugs / active metabolites","No concentration–effect relationship"]]],
{i:"TDM individualises therapy for drugs whose levels, not doses, best predict response and toxicity.",f:"Dose → plasma concentration (varies with absorption, Vd, metabolism, excretion, interactions)\n → measure at steady state (4–5 t½), trough/peak\n → compare with therapeutic range + clinical status → adjust dose",c:"Therapeutic drug monitoring guides dosing of narrow-therapeutic-index, highly variable drugs such as antiepileptics, lithium, digoxin, aminoglycosides, vancomycin and immunosuppressants; correct timing and interpretation in clinical context are essential."});

Z("pharm15",5,"ph",["Antiseptics are chemicals applied to living tissues (skin, mucosa, wounds) to kill or inhibit microbes, while disinfectants are used on inanimate objects and surfaces; examples include alcohol, chlorhexidine, povidone-iodine, hydrogen peroxide, hypochlorite, glutaraldehyde and phenols.","Antiseptics are gentle enough for skin; disinfectants are harsher and meant for floors, instruments and surfaces — the same chemical can be either depending on concentration.",["Alcohols (ethanol 70%, isopropanol 60–90%): protein denaturation, rapid; hand rub, skin prep; not sporicidal; flammable.","Chlorhexidine (0.5–4%): membrane damage, persistent action; hand wash (4%), surgical scrub, skin prep (2% in alcohol), mouthwash (0.12–0.2%), cord care (7.1% digluconate); ADR — teeth staining, ototoxicity (middle ear).","Iodine/povidone-iodine (5–10%): oxidation; broad incl. spores (slow); skin prep, wounds; ADR — irritation, hypersensitivity, thyroid effects (neonates, large areas).","Others: hydrogen peroxide 3% (wounds, mouthwash; effervescence), potassium permanganate 1:10,000 (wounds, gastric lavage), gentian violet, silver sulfadiazine/nitrate (burns, eyes), benzalkonium (cationic detergent); disinfectants — sodium hypochlorite (1% for spills, 0.5% surfaces, Ca hypochlorite water), glutaraldehyde 2% (high-level — endoscopes; sporicidal 10 h), formaldehyde (fumigation), phenol/cresol (Lysol), ethylene oxide (gas sterilisation)."],"Spores killed by: glutaraldehyde, formaldehyde, hypochlorite (high conc.), ethylene oxide, peracetic acid — not alcohol/chlorhexidine."],
"Definitions: sterilisation — complete destruction of all microorganisms including spores (autoclave, hot air, ethylene oxide, irradiation); disinfection — killing or removal of pathogenic microorganisms (except spores) from inanimate objects and surfaces; antisepsis — inhibition or destruction of microorganisms on living tissue (skin, mucous membranes, wounds); antiseptics must be non-toxic and non-irritant to tissues; disinfectants may be too toxic for tissues. Levels of disinfection (Spaulding): high-level (all microbes except large numbers of spores — glutaraldehyde, ortho-phthalaldehyde, hydrogen peroxide 6–7.5%, peracetic acid, chlorine 1000+ ppm; for semi-critical items such as endoscopes), intermediate-level (mycobacteria, most viruses, fungi — alcohols, chlorine compounds, phenolics, iodophors), low-level (vegetative bacteria, some viruses, fungi — quaternary ammonium compounds). Properties of an ideal antiseptic/disinfectant: broad-spectrum (bactericidal, sporicidal, fungicidal, virucidal), rapid and sustained action, active in presence of organic matter (pus, blood), non-irritant, non-sensitising, non-toxic, no tissue damage, no corrosion or staining, stable, cheap, pleasant odour, water-soluble. Classification and agents: (1) acids — boric acid, benzoic acid, salicylic acid, acetic acid (Pseudomonas wounds, ear); (2) alcohols — ethanol (70% optimal — water needed for protein denaturation; 95% less effective), isopropyl alcohol (60–90%); mechanism — protein denaturation and membrane disruption; rapidly bactericidal, fungicidal, virucidal (enveloped), tuberculocidal; not sporicidal, evaporates (no residual action unless combined with chlorhexidine); uses — alcohol-based hand rubs (WHO formulation — hand hygiene before/after patient contact), skin preparation before injections/venepuncture/surgery (with chlorhexidine or iodine), disinfection of thermometers and small surfaces; flammable, drying to skin, painful on wounds; (3) aldehydes — formaldehyde (formalin 37–40%; gaseous fumigation of rooms/OT, preservation of tissues, sporicidal; irritant, carcinogenic), glutaraldehyde 2% alkaline (Cidex — high-level disinfection of endoscopes, cystoscopes and heat-sensitive instruments in 20 min; sterilisation in 10 h; irritant to skin/eyes/respiratory tract), ortho-phthalaldehyde; (4) halogens — iodine: tincture iodine 2% in alcohol, Lugol iodine, povidone-iodine (iodophor 5–10% — slow-release, less irritant, less staining) — oxidises and iodinates microbial proteins; broad spectrum including spores (slowly), fungi, viruses; uses — preoperative skin preparation, surgical scrub (7.5%), wound and burn care, mouth gargles, vaginal douches, ophthalmia neonatorum prophylaxis (povidone-iodine 2.5%); ADR — local irritation, staining, hypersensitivity, systemic absorption (large wounds, neonates — hypothyroidism/hyperthyroidism, metabolic acidosis); chlorine compounds: sodium hypochlorite (household bleach 5% — diluted 1% for blood spills and biomedical waste, 0.5% for surfaces, 0.1% for instruments; Dakin solution 0.5% for wounds), calcium hypochlorite (bleaching powder — water disinfection, latrines), chloramine, chlorine gas (water supply — residual 0.5 ppm), chlorhexidine is not a halogen; mechanism — oxidation by hypochlorous acid; virucidal including HBV, HIV, coronaviruses; sporicidal at high concentration; inactivated by organic matter; corrosive to metals; (5) oxidising agents — hydrogen peroxide (3% — wound cleansing, effervescence removes debris, ear wax; 6–7.5% high-level disinfection; vaporised for room decontamination), potassium permanganate (1:4000–1:10,000 — weeping eczema, fungal infections of feet, gastric lavage in some poisonings, oxidises alkaloids), benzoyl peroxide (acne), peracetic acid (sterilant), ozone; (6) phenols — phenol (carbolic acid; Lister — standard for phenol coefficient; protoplasmic poison; caustic, toxic), cresol (Lysol — disinfection of floors, excreta), chloroxylenol (Dettol — skin and surgical antiseptic), hexachlorophene (neurotoxic in infants — restricted), triclosan; (7) biguanides — chlorhexidine gluconate (Savlon with cetrimide): cationic bisbiguanide; binds and disrupts negatively charged bacterial cell membranes (bacteriostatic at low, bactericidal at high concentration); active against Gram-positive > Gram-negative, fungi, enveloped viruses; not sporicidal or tuberculocidal; persistent residual activity on skin (binds keratin) — major advantage; not inactivated much by blood; uses — surgical hand scrub (4%), preoperative skin antisepsis (2% chlorhexidine in 70% isopropyl alcohol — superior to povidone-iodine for reducing surgical site and catheter infections), central line care, mouthwash (0.12–0.2% — gingivitis, plaque, oral care in ventilated patients), umbilical cord care (7.1% chlorhexidine digluconate gel — reduces neonatal sepsis; part of India's newborn care), obstetric and vaginal antisepsis, wound cleansing, bladder irrigation; ADR — skin irritation, rare anaphylaxis, brown staining of teeth and tongue, taste disturbance (mouthwash), ototoxicity (contraindicated in middle ear — perforated drum), keratitis (eye), neurotoxicity (meninges); incompatible with soaps and anionic detergents; (8) surface-active agents (detergents) — cationic: quaternary ammonium compounds — cetrimide, benzalkonium chloride (preservative in eye drops), cetylpyridinium (mouthwash) — low-level disinfectants, inactivated by soaps and organic matter, Pseudomonas may grow in solutions; anionic: soaps (mechanical cleaning); (9) dyes — gentian violet (crystal violet 0.5–1% — Gram-positive, Candida — oral thrush, skin infections; staining), acriflavine, proflavine, brilliant green; (10) metallic salts — silver nitrate (1% eye drops — Credé prophylaxis of ophthalmia neonatorum; 0.5% burns; caustic stick for granulation), silver sulfadiazine (burns), mercurials (thiomersal — vaccine preservative; obsolete as antiseptics), zinc oxide, calamine, copper sulphate; (11) gases — ethylene oxide (sterilisation of heat-sensitive items — plastics, catheters; toxic, explosive, carcinogenic; requires aeration), formaldehyde vapour, β-propiolactone, hydrogen peroxide plasma; (12) miscellaneous — nitrofurazone, mupirocin and fusidic acid (topical antibiotics, not antiseptics), triclosan, essential oils. Uses summary: hand hygiene (alcohol rub, chlorhexidine/soap), surgical scrub and skin preparation (chlorhexidine–alcohol, povidone-iodine), wounds and burns (povidone-iodine, hydrogen peroxide, silver sulfadiazine, Dakin solution), mucous membranes (chlorhexidine mouthwash, povidone-iodine), instruments (glutaraldehyde, hypochlorite, alcohol), surfaces and spills (hypochlorite 0.5–1%, phenolics), excreta and linen (hypochlorite, cresol), water (chlorine, bleaching powder), room fumigation (formaldehyde, hydrogen peroxide vapour), biomedical waste management (hypochlorite pre-treatment).",
[["Common antiseptics and disinfectants",[["Agent","Concentration","Spectrum","Uses"],["Ethanol / isopropanol","70% / 60–90%","Bacteria, fungi, enveloped viruses, TB; not spores","Hand rub, skin prep"],["Chlorhexidine","0.12–4% (7.1% cord)","Gram + > Gram −, fungi; persistent; not spores","Scrub, skin prep, mouthwash, cord care"],["Povidone-iodine","5–10%","Broad incl. spores (slow)","Skin prep, wounds, burns"],["Hydrogen peroxide","3% (6–7.5% HLD)","Broad","Wounds, mouthwash, HLD"],["Sodium hypochlorite","0.1–1%","Broad incl. HBV/HIV; spores (high)","Spills, surfaces, instruments, water"],["Glutaraldehyde","2%","High-level incl. spores (10 h)","Endoscopes, heat-sensitive instruments"],["Formaldehyde","Gas/formalin","Sporicidal","Fumigation, tissue preservation"],["Phenols (cresol, chloroxylenol)","Variable","Bacteria, fungi","Floors, excreta; skin (Dettol)"],["Cetrimide, benzalkonium","0.1–1%","Low-level","Wound cleaning, preservative"],["Silver nitrate / sulfadiazine","0.5–1%","Bacteria","Burns, eye prophylaxis"],["Ethylene oxide","Gas","Sterilant","Heat-sensitive items"]]],
["Definitions",[["Term","Meaning"],["Sterilisation","Kill all organisms incl. spores"],["Disinfection","Kill pathogens (not spores) on objects"],["Antisepsis","Inhibit/kill microbes on living tissue"],["High-level disinfection","All except large numbers of spores"]]]],
{c:"Antiseptics such as alcohol, chlorhexidine and povidone-iodine protect living tissues, while disinfectants like hypochlorite and glutaraldehyde decontaminate surfaces and instruments; choice depends on spectrum, persistence, tissue tolerance and organic load."});

Z("pharm15",6,"ph",["Drugs for skin and eye are mainly applied topically: dermatological agents include emollients, keratolytics, topical steroids, antimicrobials, retinoids and sunscreens; ocular drugs include mydriatics/cycloplegics (atropine, tropicamide, phenylephrine), miotics (pilocarpine), antiglaucoma drugs, antibiotics, steroids and local anaesthetics.","Topical therapy puts the drug exactly where it is needed — on the skin or eye — with high local concentration and fewer systemic effects; the vehicle (cream, ointment, drops) matters as much as the drug.",["Skin: emollients (petrolatum), keratolytics (salicylic acid, urea), topical steroids (potency classes — clobetasol super-potent → hydrocortisone mild; ADR — atrophy, striae, acne, tachyphylaxis, systemic absorption), antifungals (clotrimazole, terbinafine), antibacterials (mupirocin, fusidic acid), retinoids (tretinoin, adapalene), benzoyl peroxide, calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus), scabicides, sunscreens, coal tar, calcipotriol.","Mydriatics/cycloplegics: antimuscarinics — atropine (7–10 days; children refraction, iritis), homatropine, cyclopentolate, tropicamide (shortest, 4–6 h — fundoscopy); sympathomimetic — phenylephrine 2.5–10% (mydriasis without cycloplegia); uses — fundus exam, refraction, iridocyclitis (prevent synechiae); CI — angle-closure glaucoma.","Miotics: pilocarpine (glaucoma, reverse mydriasis); antiglaucoma — prostaglandin analogues (latanoprost — first-line), β-blockers (timolol), α2 agonists (brimonidine), carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (dorzolamide, acetazolamide), mannitol (acute).","Others: antibiotic drops (moxifloxacin, chloramphenicol, tobramycin), antivirals (acyclovir ointment, ganciclovir gel), steroids (prednisolone acetate — ↑ IOP, cataract, herpes), NSAIDs (ketorolac), local anaesthetics (proparacaine, tetracaine), artificial tears, fluorescein (diagnostic), anti-VEGF (ranibizumab — intravitreal)."],"Tropicamide = shortest mydriatic (fundoscopy); atropine = longest (iritis, children refraction)."],
"Dermatological pharmacology: percutaneous absorption depends on stratum corneum integrity (increased in inflamed/damaged skin, occlusion, hydration, thin skin of face, eyelids, genitalia, flexures and infants), drug lipophilicity, concentration and vehicle. Vehicles (bases): ointments (greasy, occlusive — dry, lichenified, scaly lesions; best penetration), creams (oil-in-water emulsions — cosmetically acceptable, moist lesions), lotions and solutions (hairy areas, weeping lesions), gels (scalp, face, acne), pastes (protective), powders (intertriginous), foams, shampoos. Classes: (1) emollients and moisturisers — liquid paraffin, white soft paraffin (petrolatum), glycerine, lanolin, urea (humectant), ceramides — dry skin, eczema, ichthyosis, psoriasis; (2) keratolytics — salicylic acid (3–6% psoriasis, warts 10–40%), urea (10–40%), lactic acid, resorcinol, sulfur, benzoyl peroxide; (3) topical corticosteroids — classified by potency: super-potent (class I: clobetasol propionate 0.05%, halobetasol), potent (betamethasone dipropionate, mometasone furoate, fluocinolone, triamcinolone 0.1%), moderate (betamethasone valerate 0.1%, fluticasone, clobetasone butyrate), mild (hydrocortisone 1%, desonide) — uses: eczema/dermatitis, psoriasis, lichen planus, discoid lupus, vitiligo, alopecia areata, insect bites, pruritus; principles — mildest effective potency for shortest time, mild steroids on face/flexures/infants, fingertip unit dosing, avoid on infections (tinea incognito), taper; adverse effects — local: skin atrophy, striae, telangiectasia, purpura, hypopigmentation, steroid acne, rosacea, perioral dermatitis, hirsutism, masking/worsening infections (tinea, scabies), delayed wound healing, contact allergy, tachyphylaxis, rebound flare (topical steroid withdrawal — misuse of OTC steroid combinations in India is common); ocular — glaucoma, cataract (eyelids); systemic — HPA axis suppression, Cushing syndrome, growth retardation in children (extensive use, occlusion); (4) topical antibacterials — mupirocin (impetigo, MRSA decolonisation of nares), fusidic acid, retapamulin, neomycin–bacitracin–polymyxin, silver sulfadiazine (burns), metronidazole (rosacea), clindamycin/erythromycin (acne); (5) antifungals — azoles (clotrimazole, miconazole, ketoconazole, luliconazole, sertaconazole), allylamines (terbinafine, butenafine), ciclopirox, amorolfine (nail lacquer), tolnaftate, Whitfield ointment (benzoic + salicylic acid), nystatin (Candida), selenium sulphide (pityriasis versicolor, dandruff); (6) antivirals — acyclovir, penciclovir creams (herpes labialis); podophyllotoxin, imiquimod (warts); (7) antiparasitics — permethrin, benzyl benzoate, crotamiton, ivermectin lotion; (8) acne drugs — benzoyl peroxide (bactericidal, comedolytic), topical retinoids (tretinoin, adapalene, tazarotene — comedolytic; photosensitivity, irritation, teratogenic avoidance), azelaic acid, topical antibiotics (with BPO to prevent resistance), dapsone gel; systemic — doxycycline, isotretinoin, OCPs, spironolactone; (9) psoriasis — coal tar, dithranol (anthralin), calcipotriol (vitamin D analogue), tazarotene, topical steroids; phototherapy (narrowband UVB, PUVA with psoralens), systemic methotrexate, cyclosporine, acitretin, apremilast, biologics; (10) immunomodulators — tacrolimus and pimecrolimus (topical calcineurin inhibitors — atopic dermatitis on face, steroid-sparing; burning), crisaborole (PDE4), ruxolitinib cream (JAK); (11) depigmenting agents — hydroquinone, azelaic acid, kojic acid, tretinoin (melasma; Kligman formula), monobenzone; repigmenting — psoralens, tacrolimus (vitiligo); (12) sunscreens — physical/inorganic (zinc oxide, titanium dioxide — reflect/scatter UV) and chemical/organic (avobenzone, oxybenzone, octinoxate — absorb UV); SPF (UVB protection), PA/broad-spectrum (UVA); uses — photodermatoses, melasma, lupus, skin cancer prevention; (13) antipruritics — calamine, menthol, camphor, crotamiton, doxepin cream, local anaesthetics (pramoxine); (14) minoxidil (androgenetic alopecia), eflornithine (hirsutism), caustics (podophyllin, trichloroacetic acid, silver nitrate). Ocular pharmacology: routes — topical drops (most common; corneal penetration favoured by lipid and water solubility, contact time; systemic absorption via nasolacrimal duct — nasolacrimal occlusion reduces), ointments (longer contact, blurred vision — night), subconjunctival, sub-Tenon, intracameral, intravitreal injections (anti-VEGF, antibiotics, steroids), systemic. Drug groups: (1) mydriatics and cycloplegics — antimuscarinics block sphincter pupillae (mydriasis) and ciliary muscle (cycloplegia — paralysis of accommodation): atropine 1% (most potent, longest — mydriasis 7–10 days, cycloplegia 7–12 days; refraction in young children with high accommodation/esotropia, iridocyclitis and corneal ulcer — relieves ciliary spasm and pain, prevents posterior synechiae, amblyopia penalisation; low-dose 0.01% for myopia control; ADR — photophobia, blurred near vision, allergic conjunctivitis, systemic toxicity in children — flushing, fever, tachycardia, delirium), homatropine 2% (1–3 days; uveitis), cyclopentolate 1% (24 h; cycloplegic refraction in children), tropicamide 0.5–1% (shortest — 4–6 h; mainly mydriatic with weak cycloplegia; routine fundoscopy); sympathomimetic mydriatics — phenylephrine 2.5–10% (α1 agonist — contracts dilator pupillae; mydriasis without cycloplegia; combined with tropicamide for maximal dilatation; hypertension risk with 10% in infants/cardiac patients), adrenaline (historical); uses of mydriatics — fundus examination (ophthalmoscopy), refraction (cycloplegic), uveitis/iridocyclitis (with steroids), breaking posterior synechiae (alternate with miotics), before cataract and vitreoretinal surgery, malignant glaucoma (atropine), penalisation therapy; contraindications — angle-closure glaucoma or narrow angles (precipitate acute attack — check depth of anterior chamber), caution in children (systemic toxicity) and elderly with BPH; reversal — dapiprazole (α-blocker) or pilocarpine (rarely used); (2) miotics — pilocarpine 1–4% (muscarinic agonist; contracts sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle — opens trabecular meshwork; acute angle-closure glaucoma (pre-laser), chronic glaucoma (now rarely), presbyopia (1.25%), reversal of mydriasis; ADR — brow ache, accommodative spasm, myopia, dim vision, retinal detachment), carbachol, physostigmine/echothiophate (obsolete); (3) antiglaucoma drugs — reduce aqueous production (β-blockers — timolol, betaxolol; α2 agonists — brimonidine, apraclonidine; carbonic anhydrase inhibitors — dorzolamide, brinzolamide topical, acetazolamide oral/IV) or increase outflow (prostaglandin F2α analogues — latanoprost, bimatoprost, travoprost: uveoscleral outflow; first-line, once daily at night; iris pigmentation, eyelash growth, periorbital fat atrophy, cystoid macular oedema; miotics; rho kinase inhibitor netarsudil — trabecular outflow); hyperosmotic agents (mannitol IV, glycerol oral) in acute angle closure; (4) anti-infectives — antibacterial drops/ointments (moxifloxacin, gatifloxacin, ciprofloxacin, ofloxacin, tobramycin, gentamicin, chloramphenicol, erythromycin, sulfacetamide, polymyxin; fortified cefazolin/vancomycin + tobramycin for bacterial keratitis), antivirals (acyclovir 3% ointment, ganciclovir 0.15% gel — herpetic keratitis; intravitreal ganciclovir — CMV retinitis), antifungals (natamycin 5% — drug of choice for filamentous fungal keratitis, common in India after vegetative trauma; voriconazole, amphotericin B 0.15%), antiprotozoal (PHMB, chlorhexidine 0.02% — Acanthamoeba keratitis); (5) anti-inflammatory — corticosteroids (prednisolone acetate 1%, dexamethasone, fluorometholone, loteprednol — uveitis, allergic conjunctivitis, postoperative inflammation; ADR — raised IOP (steroid responders — glaucoma), posterior subcapsular cataract, exacerbation of herpes simplex keratitis and fungal infections, delayed epithelial healing — never prescribe steroid drops without slit-lamp exam), NSAIDs (ketorolac, nepafenac, bromfenac, flurbiprofen — postoperative inflammation, cystoid macular oedema, prevent intraoperative miosis), mast cell stabilisers and antihistamines (sodium cromoglycate, olopatadine, ketotifen — allergic and vernal conjunctivitis), cyclosporine 0.05%/lifitegrast (dry eye); (6) local anaesthetics — proparacaine, tetracaine, lignocaine (tonometry, foreign body removal, minor procedures; not for repeated use — corneal toxicity), peribulbar/retrobulbar blocks (lignocaine + bupivacaine); (7) lubricants/artificial tears — carboxymethylcellulose, hydroxypropyl methylcellulose, polyvinyl alcohol, hyaluronate (dry eye); (8) diagnostic agents — fluorescein (corneal abrasions, angiography), rose bengal/lissamine green (dry eye), indocyanine green; (9) anti-VEGF agents — ranibizumab, aflibercept, bevacizumab, faricimab (intravitreal — neovascular AMD, diabetic macular oedema, retinal vein occlusion, ROP); verteporfin (photodynamic therapy); (10) others — botulinum toxin (strabismus, blepharospasm), mitomycin C (pterygium, trabeculectomy), riboflavin (corneal cross-linking for keratoconus), vitamin A (xerophthalmia), viscoelastics (surgery).",
[["Topical corticosteroid potency",[["Potency","Examples","Use"],["Super-potent (I)","Clobetasol propionate 0.05%","Thick psoriasis, lichen planus (short)"],["Potent (II–III)","Betamethasone dipropionate, mometasone","Severe eczema, psoriasis"],["Moderate (IV–V)","Betamethasone valerate, fluticasone","Moderate dermatoses"],["Mild (VI–VII)","Hydrocortisone 1%, desonide","Face, flexures, infants"]]],
["Mydriatics and cycloplegics",[["Drug","Class","Duration","Use"],["Atropine 1%","Antimuscarinic","7–12 days","Refraction in young children, iridocyclitis"],["Homatropine 2%","Antimuscarinic","1–3 days","Uveitis"],["Cyclopentolate 1%","Antimuscarinic","24 h","Cycloplegic refraction"],["Tropicamide 0.5–1%","Antimuscarinic","4–6 h","Fundoscopy"],["Phenylephrine 2.5–10%","α1 agonist","2–3 h","Mydriasis without cycloplegia"]]],
["Antiglaucoma drugs",[["Class","Drugs","Mechanism"],["PG analogues","Latanoprost, bimatoprost","↑ Uveoscleral outflow (first-line)"],["β-blockers","Timolol, betaxolol","↓ Aqueous production"],["α2 agonists","Brimonidine","↓ Production, ↑ outflow"],["CAIs","Dorzolamide, acetazolamide","↓ Production"],["Miotics","Pilocarpine","↑ Trabecular outflow"],["Hyperosmotics","Mannitol, glycerol","↓ Vitreous volume (acute)"]]],
["Ocular steroid adverse effects",["Raised IOP (steroid glaucoma)","Posterior subcapsular cataract","Exacerbation of herpes and fungal keratitis","Delayed epithelial healing"]]],
{i:"Topical therapy for skin and eye delivers high local drug levels; correct choice of potency, vehicle and duration prevents local and systemic toxicity.",f:"Pupil: sphincter (M3 — parasympathetic) ← atropine blocks → mydriasis + cycloplegia\n Dilator (α1 — sympathetic) ← phenylephrine stimulates → mydriasis only\n Pilocarpine (M3 agonist) → miosis, ↑ trabecular outflow",c:"Dermatological drugs range from emollients and keratolytics to potency-graded topical steroids, antimicrobials and retinoids, while ocular drugs include antimuscarinic and sympathomimetic mydriatics, miotics, antiglaucoma agents, anti-infectives and steroids — each requiring awareness of local adverse effects such as skin atrophy or steroid-induced glaucoma."});

Z("pharm15",7,"ph",["Vaccines produce active immunity by exposing the body to antigens (live attenuated, inactivated, subunit, toxoid, conjugate, mRNA or viral vector); sera (antitoxins, antivenoms) and immunoglobulins provide immediate but short-lived passive immunity.","A vaccine is a training drill that teaches the immune system to make its own long-lasting defences; antisera and immunoglobulins are borrowed ready-made antibodies — instant but temporary.",["Active immunisation: live attenuated (BCG, OPV, MR, rotavirus, yellow fever, varicella), inactivated (IPV, rabies, hepatitis A, influenza, Covaxin), subunit/recombinant (hepatitis B, HPV), toxoids (tetanus, diphtheria), conjugate (Hib, PCV, typhoid conjugate, MenACWY), mRNA/vector (COVID-19).","UIP India: BCG, OPV-0, hep B birth dose; 6/10/14 weeks — pentavalent (DPT-HepB-Hib), OPV, rotavirus, fIPV (6, 14 wk), PCV; 9 months — MR, JE (endemic), PCV booster; 16–24 months — DPT, OPV, MR boosters; 5–6 yr DPT; 10 & 16 yr Td; pregnant women Td; HPV (girls 9–14, from 2024 campaigns).","Passive immunisation: antitoxins/antisera (diphtheria antitoxin, antisnake venom — polyvalent equine, anti-rabies serum — ERIG, anti-tetanus serum — ATS); risk of serum sickness/anaphylaxis — test dose, adrenaline ready.","Human immunoglobulins: normal IVIG (immunodeficiency, ITP, Kawasaki, GBS), specific — HRIG (rabies category III), TIG (tetanus), HBIG (newborn of HBsAg+ mother, needle-stick), VZIG, anti-D (Rh-negative mother within 72 h); monoclonals (palivizumab — RSV)."],"Live vaccines: avoid in pregnancy and immunosuppression; give together or 4 weeks apart."],
"Immunisation may be active (stimulating the host's immune system to produce antibodies and memory cells — slow onset 2–4 weeks, long-lasting) or passive (transfer of preformed antibodies — immediate, short-lived protection of weeks to months, no memory). Vaccines (active immunisation): (1) live attenuated vaccines — BCG, oral polio (OPV — Sabin; bivalent), measles, mumps, rubella (MR/MMR), rotavirus (Rotavac, Rotasiil), varicella, yellow fever, live attenuated influenza, typhoid Ty21a, cholera (oral), Japanese encephalitis (SA 14-14-2 live), smallpox (historical), dengue (CYD-TDV, TAK-003); advantages — single dose often sufficient, mimic natural infection, humoral and cellular (and mucosal — OPV) immunity; disadvantages — reversion to virulence (vaccine-derived polio), contraindicated in pregnancy and immunodeficiency, cold chain sensitivity; (2) inactivated (killed) whole-organism vaccines — inactivated polio (IPV — Salk; fractional intradermal fIPV), rabies (cell culture — PCECV, HDCV, PVRV), hepatitis A, influenza (inactivated split/subunit), Japanese encephalitis (inactivated Vero cell), whole-cell pertussis, Covaxin (COVID-19), cholera (killed oral — Shanchol), typhoid (whole-cell — obsolete); require multiple doses and boosters, safe in immunocompromised; (3) subunit, recombinant and polysaccharide vaccines — hepatitis B (recombinant HBsAg), HPV (VLP — quadrivalent, 9-valent, Cervavac — Indian), acellular pertussis, Vi polysaccharide typhoid, pneumococcal polysaccharide (PPSV23), meningococcal polysaccharide, recombinant zoster (Shingrix), malaria (RTS,S, R21), Corbevax (COVID-19 protein subunit); polysaccharide vaccines are T-independent (poor in < 2 years, no memory); (4) conjugate vaccines — polysaccharide linked to protein carrier (tetanus toxoid, CRM197) to induce T-dependent response in infants: Hib, pneumococcal conjugate (PCV10/13/15/20), meningococcal conjugate (MenACWY, MenAfriVac), typhoid conjugate vaccine (Vi-TT); (5) toxoids — inactivated exotoxins: tetanus toxoid (TT/Td), diphtheria toxoid (in DPT, DT, Td); (6) newer platforms — mRNA (COVID-19 Pfizer, Moderna; Gennova's GEMCOVAC), viral vector (ChAdOx1 — Covishield, Sputnik), DNA (ZyCoV-D — first DNA vaccine), virus-like particles; (7) combination vaccines — DPT, pentavalent (DPT + HepB + Hib), hexavalent (+ IPV), MMR, MR. Universal Immunisation Programme (UIP, India; Mission Indradhanush; U-WIN digital platform) national schedule: at birth — BCG, OPV-0 (within 15 days), hepatitis B birth dose (within 24 h); 6 weeks — OPV-1, pentavalent-1, rotavirus-1, fIPV-1, PCV-1; 10 weeks — OPV-2, pentavalent-2, rotavirus-2; 14 weeks — OPV-3, pentavalent-3, rotavirus-3, fIPV-2, PCV-2; 9–12 months — MR-1, JE-1 (endemic districts), PCV booster, vitamin A (1st dose), fIPV-3 (recently added at 9 months); 16–24 months — MR-2, JE-2, DPT booster-1, OPV booster, vitamin A (2nd, then every 6 months to 5 years); 5–6 years — DPT booster-2; 10 years and 16 years — Td; pregnant women — Td-1 early in pregnancy, Td-2 four weeks later (or Td booster if vaccinated within last 3 years); HPV vaccine for girls 9–14 years (national introduction); adult JE in endemic areas; COVID-19 vaccination; typhoid conjugate vaccine in some states. Vaccine storage: cold chain 2–8 °C (ILR, deep freezers for OPV at −15 to −25 °C historically), vaccine vial monitor (VVM), open vial policy, shake test for freeze-sensitive vaccines (DPT, TT, Hep B, PCV). General contraindications: anaphylaxis to previous dose or component; live vaccines — pregnancy, severe immunodeficiency (congenital, HIV with low CD4, high-dose steroids ≥ 2 mg/kg or ≥ 20 mg/day for ≥ 14 days, chemotherapy, radiotherapy), recent immunoglobulin or blood products (wait 3–11 months for measles vaccine); acute moderate–severe illness (defer; minor illness not a contraindication); pertussis — encephalopathy within 7 days of previous dose (use DT). Adverse events following immunisation (AEFI): minor — local pain, swelling, fever, irritability; serious — anaphylaxis (keep adrenaline 1:1000 ready, observe 30 min), seizures, hypotonic-hyporesponsive episodes (DPT), vaccine-associated paralytic polio (OPV), intussusception (rotavirus — rare), BCG lymphadenitis, toxic shock syndrome (programme error — reconstituted vial contamination), Guillain–Barré (influenza — rare), thrombosis with thrombocytopenia (adenoviral vector COVID), myocarditis (mRNA); reporting through AEFI surveillance system. Sera (antisera, heterologous antibodies): prepared by hyperimmunising horses (equine) with toxins or antigens; contain specific immunoglobulins (refined, enzyme-digested F(ab')2 fragments to reduce reactions); examples — diphtheria antitoxin (ADS — 20,000–100,000 U IM/IV for diphtheria; given immediately on clinical suspicion), anti-tetanus serum (ATS — 1500 U prophylaxis; largely replaced by human TIG), antisnake venom (polyvalent ASV — Indian 'big four': cobra, common krait, Russell's viper, saw-scaled viper; 8–10 vials IV in severe envenomation, repeated per clotting test/neurotoxicity), anti-rabies serum (ERIG 40 IU/kg infiltrated into wounds), anti-scorpion venom (prazosin preferred), gas gangrene antitoxin (obsolete), botulinum antitoxin; risks — immediate hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis (skin/conjunctival test dose not reliable — premedication controversial; adrenaline ready), serum sickness (fever, rash, arthralgia, lymphadenopathy, proteinuria 7–12 days later — antihistamines, steroids), sensitisation (repeated use). Human immunoglobulins (homologous): pooled normal human immunoglobulin (IVIG/SCIG — IgG from thousands of donors): uses — primary immunodeficiency (agammaglobulinaemia, CVID) replacement, immune thrombocytopenic purpura, Kawasaki disease (2 g/kg with aspirin — prevents coronary aneurysms), Guillain–Barré syndrome, myasthenic crisis, CIDP, dermatomyositis, multifocal motor neuropathy, toxic epidermal necrolysis (controversial), hepatitis A and measles post-exposure prophylaxis (IM); adverse effects — infusion reactions (headache, flushing, chills), aseptic meningitis, renal failure (sucrose-containing), thrombosis, haemolysis, anaphylaxis in IgA-deficient patients with anti-IgA antibodies; specific (hyperimmune) human immunoglobulins: human rabies immunoglobulin (HRIG 20 IU/kg — category III bites, infiltrated into and around wound on day 0 along with vaccine; rabies monoclonal antibodies — Rabishield, Twinrab — alternatives), tetanus immunoglobulin (TIG 250–500 IU IM prophylaxis in tetanus-prone wounds of unimmunised; 3000–6000 IU treatment), hepatitis B immunoglobulin (HBIG 0.5 mL within 12 h for newborns of HBsAg-positive mothers with vaccine; post-exposure after needle-stick, sexual exposure, liver transplant), varicella-zoster immunoglobulin (VZIG — immunocompromised, pregnant women, neonates exposed to varicella), anti-D (Rho(D)) immunoglobulin (300 µg IM to Rh-negative unsensitised mothers within 72 h of delivery of Rh-positive baby, abortion, amniocentesis, antepartum haemorrhage, at 28 weeks — prevents haemolytic disease of newborn; also ITP), cytomegalovirus immunoglobulin, botulism immunoglobulin (infant botulism); monoclonal antibodies for passive immunisation — palivizumab and nirsevimab (RSV prophylaxis in high-risk infants), rabies mAbs, COVID-19 mAbs (historical), bezlotoxumab (C. difficile recurrence). Combined active–passive immunisation: rabies category III (HRIG/ERIG + vaccine), tetanus-prone wound in unimmunised (TIG + TT at different sites), hepatitis B exposure in newborn (HBIG + vaccine), diphtheria (antitoxin + toxoid after recovery).",
[["Types of vaccines",[["Type","Examples","Features"],["Live attenuated","BCG, OPV, MR/MMR, rotavirus, varicella, yellow fever","Strong immunity; CI pregnancy, immunodeficiency"],["Inactivated","IPV, rabies, hepatitis A, influenza, Covaxin","Safe; boosters needed"],["Subunit/recombinant","Hepatitis B, HPV, acellular pertussis, Corbevax","Safe; adjuvants"],["Polysaccharide","PPSV23, Vi typhoid","Poor < 2 years"],["Conjugate","Hib, PCV, TCV, MenACWY","Effective in infants"],["Toxoid","Tetanus, diphtheria","Antitoxin immunity"],["mRNA/vector/DNA","COVID-19 (mRNA, Covishield, ZyCoV-D)","New platforms"]]],
["UIP schedule (India)",[["Age","Vaccines"],["Birth","BCG, OPV-0, Hep B birth dose"],["6 weeks","OPV-1, Penta-1, RVV-1, fIPV-1, PCV-1"],["10 weeks","OPV-2, Penta-2, RVV-2"],["14 weeks","OPV-3, Penta-3, RVV-3, fIPV-2, PCV-2"],["9–12 months","MR-1, JE-1, PCV booster, fIPV-3, vitamin A"],["16–24 months","MR-2, JE-2, DPT-B1, OPV-B, vitamin A"],["5–6 years","DPT-B2"],["10 & 16 years","Td"],["Pregnancy","Td-1, Td-2 / booster"]]],
["Passive immunisation",[["Product","Use"],["Diphtheria antitoxin (equine)","Diphtheria"],["Polyvalent ASV (equine)","Snakebite (big four)"],["ERIG / HRIG / rabies mAb","Rabies category III"],["TIG (human)","Tetanus prophylaxis/treatment"],["HBIG","Newborn of HBsAg+ mother, needle-stick"],["VZIG","Varicella exposure in high-risk"],["Anti-D","Rh-negative mother within 72 h"],["IVIG","Immunodeficiency, ITP, Kawasaki, GBS"],["Palivizumab/nirsevimab","RSV prophylaxis"]]]],
{i:"India's UIP is among the world's largest immunisation programmes; vaccines and passive immunisation products together prevent and treat many life-threatening infections.",f:"Active immunity: vaccine (antigen) → B/T cell activation → antibodies + memory (weeks; long-lasting)\n Passive immunity: preformed antibodies (antisera, Ig) → immediate protection (weeks–months; no memory)\n Combined: rabies (RIG + vaccine), tetanus (TIG + TT), hepatitis B newborn (HBIG + vaccine)",c:"Vaccines of various types confer long-lasting active immunity delivered through the UIP schedule, while heterologous sera and human immunoglobulins give immediate passive protection — often combined with vaccines in post-exposure situations such as rabies, tetanus and hepatitis B."});

Z("pharm15",8,"ph",["OTC (over-the-counter) drugs can be bought without prescription for self-care of minor ailments; generic drugs are bioequivalent, cheaper copies of off-patent branded drugs; pharmacoeconomics compares costs and outcomes of drug therapies to guide value-based choices.","OTC drugs are 'self-help' medicines; generics are the same medicine without the brand price; pharmacoeconomics asks 'is the benefit worth the money?'.",["OTC drugs: safe, wide margin, for self-limiting conditions (paracetamol, antacids, ORS, cetirizine, topical antifungals, nicotine gum); India — no formal OTC category yet (proposed list); risks — misuse, masking disease, interactions, antibiotic misuse; role of pharmacist.","Generic vs branded: generics contain same active ingredient, strength, dosage form, route; bioequivalence (90% CI of AUC and Cmax within 80–125%); differ in name, excipients, appearance, price; branded generics in India; NMC mandates generic prescribing; Jan Aushadhi stores; concerns — quality variability, NTI drugs.","Pharmacoeconomics types: cost-minimisation (equal outcomes — cheapest), cost-effectiveness (cost per natural unit, ICER), cost-utility (cost per QALY/DALY), cost-benefit (both in money); costs — direct medical, direct non-medical, indirect (productivity), intangible.","Applications: formulary/EML decisions, NLEM, health technology assessment (HTAIn), insurance (PM-JAY), pricing (NPPA)."],"Pharmacoeconomic analyses: CMA, CEA, CUA (QALY), CBA (money)."],
"Over-the-counter (OTC) drugs (non-prescription medicines) are medicines that can be legally sold to consumers without a prescription for self-medication of minor, self-limiting conditions that the consumer can self-diagnose. Characteristics: established safety and efficacy, wide therapeutic margin, low potential for misuse/abuse and dependence, few interactions, adequate labelling and instructions for lay use, conditions not requiring medical diagnosis or monitoring. Examples: analgesic–antipyretics (paracetamol, low-dose ibuprofen, aspirin), antacids and alginates, H2 blockers (low dose), ORS, laxatives (ispaghula, lactulose), antidiarrhoeals (loperamide in adults), antihistamines (cetirizine, chlorpheniramine), decongestants (xylometazoline nasal drops — short term), cough lozenges, topical antiseptics, antifungals (clotrimazole, terbinafine cream), emollients, vitamins and minerals, nicotine replacement therapy, emergency contraceptive pill (levonorgestrel — available OTC in India), condoms, oral contraceptives (Mala-D — social marketing). Regulatory status in India: drugs are classified in the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules 1945 under schedules — Schedule H (prescription only), Schedule H1 (antibiotics including third/fourth-generation cephalosporins, carbapenems, anti-TB drugs, habit-forming drugs — require prescription records), Schedule X (narcotics and psychotropics — strict control), Schedule G (caution — under medical supervision), Schedule K (exemptions — household remedies); India has no formal legal definition of OTC; 'non-scheduled' drugs are sold without prescription; draft OTC policy proposes a list of medicines permitted for OTC sale. Benefits: convenience, empowerment and self-care, reduced burden on health system and costs, quicker relief; risks: incorrect self-diagnosis and delay in treatment of serious disease, inappropriate use, overdose (paracetamol hepatotoxicity), adverse effects and drug interactions, misuse of prescription drugs sold without prescription (antibiotics — antimicrobial resistance; steroids; sedatives), irrational FDCs, masking symptoms, dependence (codeine syrups, laxatives, nasal decongestants — rhinitis medicamentosa); role of pharmacists in counselling and of doctors in educating patients. Generic versus branded drugs: a generic drug (in strict sense) is a pharmaceutical product, usually intended to be interchangeable with an innovator (originator) product, manufactured without a licence from the innovator company and marketed after expiry of patent or exclusivity rights, sold under its International Non-proprietary Name (INN; e.g., 'atorvastatin tablets'); 'branded generics' (common in India) are generic copies sold under a brand name by companies; innovator brand — the first patented product (e.g., Lipitor). Requirements for generics: same active pharmaceutical ingredient, strength, dosage form and route as the reference product; pharmaceutical equivalence; bioequivalence — demonstrated by comparative pharmacokinetic study in healthy volunteers showing that the 90% confidence intervals for the ratio (test/reference) of AUC and Cmax fall within 80–125%; manufactured under Good Manufacturing Practice (Schedule M); may differ in excipients, colour, shape, size, packaging and price. Advantages of generics: substantially lower cost (no R&D and promotional costs; competition) — improved affordability, access and adherence; supported by NMC regulations mandating prescribing in generic names (2016/2023), Jan Aushadhi Kendras (PMBJP — quality-tested generics at 50–90% lower prices), government procurement; concerns — perceived and sometimes real variability in quality (spurious and substandard drugs; need for strong regulation and testing), bioequivalence not mandatory historically for all generics in India (now required for many), excipient-related reactions, switching between brands for narrow therapeutic index drugs (antiepileptics, levothyroxine, warfarin, cyclosporine, lithium) may cause variability — consistency advised, pharmacist substitution and patient confusion due to different appearance, influence of trade margins. Biosimilars — highly similar versions of biological products (insulin, monoclonal antibodies, erythropoietin) — require comparative clinical data, not just bioequivalence. Pharmacoeconomics: a branch of health economics that identifies, measures and compares the costs (resources consumed) and consequences (clinical, economic, humanistic outcomes) of pharmaceutical products and services, to aid decision-making on allocation of limited resources. Costs: direct medical (drugs, investigations, hospital stay, procedures, professional fees), direct non-medical (travel, food, lodging, caregiver costs), indirect (productivity loss due to morbidity and mortality, absenteeism), intangible (pain, suffering, anxiety — hard to quantify); perspective (patient, payer/insurer, hospital, society). Types of pharmacoeconomic evaluation: (1) cost-minimisation analysis (CMA) — used when two or more interventions have proven equivalent outcomes (e.g., bioequivalent generics, different routes of the same drug); simply compares costs to choose the cheapest; (2) cost-effectiveness analysis (CEA) — compares interventions with outcomes measured in the same natural units (life-years gained, mmHg BP reduction, cases cured, infections prevented); expressed as cost per unit of outcome and incremental cost-effectiveness ratio (ICER = Δcost/Δeffect); most common; (3) cost-utility analysis (CUA) — special form of CEA with outcomes measured in utilities combining quantity and quality of life — quality-adjusted life years (QALYs) or disability-adjusted life years (DALYs); allows comparison across different diseases; willingness-to-pay threshold (WHO: 1–3 × GDP per capita per DALY averted; India's HTAIn uses 1 × GDP per capita per QALY); (4) cost-benefit analysis (CBA) — both costs and outcomes are valued in monetary terms; expressed as net benefit or benefit–cost ratio; allows comparison with non-health investments but valuing life in money is difficult; also cost-of-illness studies, budget impact analysis, cost-consequence analysis, decision analysis and Markov modelling, sensitivity analysis (to test uncertainty). Applications: selection of drugs for essential medicines lists and hospital formularies, standard treatment guidelines, health technology assessment (Health Technology Assessment in India — HTAIn under DHR; recommendations for Ayushman Bharat PM-JAY packages, vaccine introduction — rotavirus, HPV), drug pricing and reimbursement (NPPA), evaluating new drugs (value of expensive biologics), guiding national programmes (TB, HIV regimens), clinical decision-making with cost awareness, reducing out-of-pocket expenditure (a major cause of impoverishment in India, mainly due to medicines).",
[["Pharmacoeconomic analyses",[["Type","Outcome measure","When used"],["Cost-minimisation (CMA)","Outcomes equal; compare costs","Bioequivalent generics, routes"],["Cost-effectiveness (CEA)","Natural units (life-years, cures, mmHg); ICER","Same outcome, different efficacy"],["Cost-utility (CUA)","QALY / DALY","Compare across diseases; HTA"],["Cost-benefit (CBA)","Money (benefit–cost ratio)","Compare with non-health investments"]]],
["Generic vs branded",[["Feature","Generic","Innovator brand"],["Active ingredient, strength, route","Same","Same"],["Bioequivalence","80–125% CI (AUC, Cmax)","Reference"],["Excipients, appearance","May differ","—"],["Cost","Much lower","High"],["Name","INN (or brand generic)","Trade name"],["Concerns","Quality variability; NTI drug switching","Cost"]]],
["OTC drugs",[["Benefits","Risks"],["Convenience, self-care","Wrong self-diagnosis, delay"],["Reduced health-system load","Overdose (paracetamol), interactions"],["Lower cost","Antibiotic misuse, AMR"],["Quick relief","Dependence (codeine, decongestants)"]]]],
{c:"OTC medicines enable safe self-care for minor ailments when well regulated, generics provide bioequivalent therapy at lower cost, and pharmacoeconomic analyses (CMA, CEA, CUA, CBA) guide value-based selection of medicines in formularies, programmes and insurance schemes."});
